WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.712
<c.p>[Begin 4482.

2
00:00:00.712 --> 00:00:01.780
Begin Session II.]

3
00:00:01.780 --> 00:00:02.492
<v CHELSEA ARSENEAULT>All right.

4
00:00:02.492 --> 00:00:03.915
So we are on.

5
00:00:03.915 --> 00:00:06.051
And today is June thirtieth, 2015.

6
00:00:06.051 --> 00:00:12.102
We’re at the Calcasieu Parish Public Library in a private room, talking again with Ms. Myrtle Rosamore.

7
00:00:12.102 --> 00:00:15.305
I think the last time we talked was January?

8
00:00:15.305 --> 00:00:16.017
<v MYRTLE MARIE ROSAMORE>Mm hmm.

9
00:00:16.017 --> 00:00:19.932
<v ARSENEAULT>At the Oral History Day at the Rigmaiden Center [Mossville, Louisiana]?

10
00:00:19.932 --> 00:00:20.288
<v ROSAMORE>Yeah.

11
00:00:20.288 --> 00:00:21.000
Yes.

12
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:21.337
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

13
00:00:21.337 --> 00:00:24.365
I couldn’t remember if it was January or March.

14
00:00:24.365 --> 00:00:26.385
But I think it was January.

15
00:00:26.385 --> 00:00:27.394
<v ROSAMORE>It was January.

16
00:00:27.394 --> 00:00:28.740
Well, let me see.

17
00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:33.115
I had moved over to, I think it was before I moved here.

18
00:00:33.115 --> 00:00:35.135
Because I moved here in March.

19
00:00:35.135 --> 00:00:36.817
It could have been March.

20
00:00:36.817 --> 00:00:37.827
<v ARSENEAULT>So either January—

21
00:00:37.827 --> 00:00:38.500
<v ROSAMORE>Or March.

22
00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:39.173
<v ARSENEAULT>Or March.

23
00:00:39.173 --> 00:00:39.510
Okay.

24
00:00:39.510 --> 00:00:41.192
I’ll have to find out.

25
00:00:41.192 --> 00:00:46.240
And the last time we talked, you shared so much about growing up in Mossville,

26
00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:51.962
your family history, feeding the family animals, your cousin getting stung in the eye with a wasp.

27
00:00:51.962 --> 00:00:52.635
<v ROSAMORE>Yeah. [laughs]

28
00:00:52.635 --> 00:00:56.000
<v ARSENEAULT>And remembering when the Old Spanish Trail was paved.

29
00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:56.330
<v ROSAMORE>Yeah.

30
00:00:56.330 --> 00:00:57.319
It was gravel.

31
00:00:57.319 --> 00:00:58.307
<v ARSENEAULT>It was gravel.

32
00:00:58.307 --> 00:00:58.637
<v ROSAMORE>Yeah.

33
00:00:58.637 --> 00:01:01.274
<v ARSENEAULT>So we want to talk more about that.

34
00:01:01.274 --> 00:01:08.856
So the first question I wanted to ask you, because we’re trying to figure it out, what are the geographic boundaries of Mossville?

35
00:01:08.856 --> 00:01:09.844
<v ROSAMORE>Let me see.

36
00:01:09.844 --> 00:01:13.800
Well, it all depends on as what are you actually looking for.

37
00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:15.448
You know what I’m saying?

38
00:01:15.448 --> 00:01:17.756
Because I pretty much grew up there,

39
00:01:17.756 --> 00:01:24.348
so it’s kind of like I didn’t really realize Mossville was Mossville till I was about maybe seven years old.

40
00:01:24.348 --> 00:01:31.600
And I remember, I just remember my dad taking me everywhere he went to go feed his little animals, his ducks and

41
00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:32.259
<c.p>all that.

42
00:01:32.259 --> 00:01:37.863
But as far as the geographical part, I’m not really sure, because I don't remember that much.

43
00:01:37.863 --> 00:01:46.104
But as I got older, you know, well, when you got older, I was in my twenties, I didn’t look at Mossville as being historic.

44
00:01:46.104 --> 00:01:48.741
I was just so busy raising my kids.

45
00:01:48.741 --> 00:01:50.389
You know what I’m saying?

46
00:01:50.389 --> 00:01:52.696
That’s what I was focusing on then.

47
00:01:52.696 --> 00:01:59.619
Trying to get an education so that if I did have kids I would be able to take care of them.

48
00:01:59.619 --> 00:02:02.585
And going to different jobs, trying to make money.

49
00:02:02.585 --> 00:02:08.848
But at one point, I didn’t really have to work and my dad and my mom had me spoiled.

50
00:02:08.848 --> 00:02:13.793
So I was kind of like just doing it because everybody else was doing it.

51
00:02:13.793 --> 00:02:15.441
You know what I mean?

52
00:02:15.441 --> 00:02:20.056
But not really trying to make a living, because I didn’t really have to.

53
00:02:20.056 --> 00:02:25.000
So I don't know what you’re really wanting to know as far as geographical.

54
00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:29.046
<v ARSENEAULT>Like they said like maybe from the railroad track to Perkinstown.

55
00:02:29.046 --> 00:02:29.782
<v ROSAMORE>Oh, Perkinstown.

56
00:02:29.782 --> 00:02:34.564
That would be like, I guess that would be going toward Mr. Virgil.

57
00:02:34.564 --> 00:02:35.668
<v ARSENEAULT>I’m not sure.

58
00:02:35.668 --> 00:02:36.404
<v ROSAMORE>That area.

59
00:02:36.404 --> 00:02:41.186
See, because I’m not familiar with that part, you know what I’m saying?

60
00:02:41.186 --> 00:02:46.336
All I remember was Old Spanish Trail has always been call Old Spanish Trail.

61
00:02:46.336 --> 00:02:52.589
Now I spoke to one of my dad’s cousins that was in his eighties before he died.

62
00:02:52.589 --> 00:02:56.636
And he told me that Mossville was once called Shoats’ Prairie.

63
00:02:56.636 --> 00:03:00.682
And he said all the Indians lived along the rivers there.

64
00:03:00.682 --> 00:03:02.521
That was before my time.

65
00:03:02.521 --> 00:03:07.671
And I guess, because he was in his eighties, so it must have been

66
00:03:07.671 --> 00:03:10.246
<c.p>when he was a young boy, maybe?

67
00:03:10.246 --> 00:03:18.707
And a lot of things you know that he told me about was back in his days, that I wouldn’t have never knew.

68
00:03:18.707 --> 00:03:20.546
You know what I’m saying?

69
00:03:20.546 --> 00:03:23.489
Now Perkinstown, I’m not sure where Perkinstown is.

70
00:03:23.489 --> 00:03:27.536
I don't know if that’s where my dad’s mother’s family lived?

71
00:03:27.536 --> 00:03:31.582
Because they had Perkinses live further down, like going towards Sulphur.

72
00:03:31.582 --> 00:03:34.525
And that was my grandma’s people as well.

73
00:03:34.525 --> 00:03:37.468
So I guess that would be called Perkinstown.

74
00:03:37.468 --> 00:03:39.675
Because it was called different areas.

75
00:03:39.675 --> 00:03:44.457
Because where I lived, on Evergreen was called, that area there was Mossville.

76
00:03:44.457 --> 00:03:48.504
Then you had going towards Westlake area was called Bel Air.

77
00:03:48.504 --> 00:03:55.493
And going towards the tracks, going like you’re going toward Highway 90, that was called, that’s still called Queensboro.

78
00:03:55.493 --> 00:04:01.379
So it was like three little areas it was called, but everybody was originally from Mossville.

79
00:04:01.379 --> 00:04:07.264
<v ARSENEAULT>That’s what we were wondering, because they had a bunch of different names for different subdivisions.

80
00:04:07.264 --> 00:04:08.000
<v ROSAMORE>Yeah.

81
00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:14.222
<v ARSENEAULT>And we’re like well where, exactly, is, because they would say Edgerly or Bel Air or Saprack.

82
00:04:14.222 --> 00:04:16.052
And we’re like, where’s Mossville?

83
00:04:16.052 --> 00:04:18.613
<v ROSAMORE>Well, it was really all of Mossville.

84
00:04:18.613 --> 00:04:21.175
But it was just named different areas.

85
00:04:21.175 --> 00:04:23.371
Like where we lived was Mossville.

86
00:04:23.371 --> 00:04:28.861
But where you go down Prater Road and all the houses back there was Queensboro.

87
00:04:28.861 --> 00:04:38.010
And then you go down OST [Old Spanish Trail] and go towards Westlake, they had a subdivision behind the school that was called Bel Air.

88
00:04:38.010 --> 00:04:39.840
And so it was like

89
00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:42.036
<c.p>three, three parts that I knew.

90
00:04:42.036 --> 00:04:47.892
But now, like I said, when I talked to my daddy’s cousin that was still alive,

91
00:04:47.892 --> 00:04:52.649
he was eighty-something, he told me that Mossville once was called Shoats’ Prairie.

92
00:04:52.649 --> 00:04:58.505
And he said they used to have a post office that was called Moss’ post office.

93
00:04:58.505 --> 00:05:03.263
Well, I never lived to see that, you know, I wasn’t even born.

94
00:05:03.263 --> 00:05:08.387
But apparently it had all the little things that a little town would have.

95
00:05:08.387 --> 00:05:11.680
But then somewhere in there, they lost all that.

96
00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:13.510
Or something happened with that.

97
00:05:13.510 --> 00:05:14.608
I don't know.

98
00:05:14.608 --> 00:05:19.000
Because when I was born, there was no post office there.

99
00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:26.412
<v ARSENEAULT>So it was there maybe back in like the 1900s and then you all never had one again?

100
00:05:26.412 --> 00:05:26.824
<v ROSAMORE>No.

101
00:05:26.824 --> 00:05:27.235
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

102
00:05:27.235 --> 00:05:31.765
<v ROSAMORE>We was pretty much unincorporated as long as I can remember,

103
00:05:31.765 --> 00:05:40.000
which mean that we was a little town sitting right there, but we was not affiliated with Westlake nor Sulphur.

104
00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:42.471
We was our own little town.

105
00:05:42.471 --> 00:05:45.353
Kind of like where I’m living now.

106
00:05:45.353 --> 00:05:47.824
It’s like in the LeBleu Settlement.

107
00:05:47.824 --> 00:05:57.294
We are, our address is always Lake Charles and sometimes Iowa, but we are not really with them, you know what I mean?

108
00:05:57.294 --> 00:06:00.176
Like Lake Charles [Iowa?], we’re not connected.

109
00:06:00.176 --> 00:06:03.882
We’re just our own little community, like Mossville was.

110
00:06:03.882 --> 00:06:06.765
Like we got our water from Mossville.

111
00:06:06.765 --> 00:06:08.000
And so—

112
00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:10.643
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you all ever try to become incorporated?

113
00:06:10.643 --> 00:06:14.938
<v ROSAMORE>No. Well, actually, it was always that, even when my dad was living.

114
00:06:14.938 --> 00:06:19.562
And when his dad was living, there was never a cooperating level of community.

115
00:06:19.562 --> 00:06:24.518
It’s like the plants can come and build by us and we had no say.

116
00:06:24.518 --> 00:06:27.161
That’s what I mean when I say unincorporated.

117
00:06:27.161 --> 00:06:33.107
Anything could have came in our area and do anything they wanted and we couldn’t say a word.

118
00:06:33.107 --> 00:06:33.768
<v ARSENEAULT>I see.

119
00:06:33.768 --> 00:06:35.420
<v ROSAMORE>You know what I mean?

120
00:06:35.420 --> 00:06:39.054
Because the plants have been around us for a long time.

121
00:06:39.054 --> 00:06:43.348
I mean, that’s what we grew up knowing, you know what I’m saying?

122
00:06:43.348 --> 00:06:45.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Had always been there.

123
00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:45.625
<v ROSAMORE>Mm hmm.

124
00:06:45.625 --> 00:06:46.562
Always been there.

125
00:06:46.562 --> 00:06:47.812
<v ARSENEAULT>Like your early memories.

126
00:06:47.812 --> 00:06:49.375
How has the community changed?

127
00:06:49.375 --> 00:06:54.062
Like how have you seen it change, like from when you were a little girl?

128
00:06:54.062 --> 00:06:55.000
Like its appearance.

129
00:06:55.000 --> 00:06:55.625
<v ROSAMORE>Oh, lord.

130
00:06:55.625 --> 00:07:01.562
From when I was little, I remember me and my little cousins used to sit out in the yard.

131
00:07:01.562 --> 00:07:05.625
And we would actually have, they had this game we used to play.

132
00:07:05.625 --> 00:07:11.875
We used to count the cars and say this is my car, this is my car, this is my car.

133
00:07:11.875 --> 00:07:14.375
Well, it was like it would take forever.

134
00:07:14.375 --> 00:07:19.688
By the time I say, this is my car, the next car pass, it would take forever.

135
00:07:19.688 --> 00:07:23.438
And then you forget who said the last one was my car.

136
00:07:23.438 --> 00:07:25.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Because you don’t see cars.

137
00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:28.438
<v ROSAMORE>Oh, it was like hours before we would see a car.

138
00:07:28.438 --> 00:07:34.375
And all of a sudden, I guess whenever the trailer park opened, then it was like a busy street.

139
00:07:34.375 --> 00:07:38.750
It went from a gravel road to a paved top, a black top road.

140
00:07:38.750 --> 00:07:43.125
It went from where we was like totally country, you know what I’m saying?

141
00:07:43.125 --> 00:07:47.812
You’d see cars every now and then, to where we start seeing cars a lot.

142
00:07:47.812 --> 00:07:51.562
We used to go hunting in the back of our houses easily.

143
00:07:51.562 --> 00:08:00.000
And then all of a sudden, people start buying out the property for the dirt pits and all that, so we couldn’t go hunting no more.

144
00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:01.477
<v ARSENEAULT>What would you all hunt?

145
00:08:01.477 --> 00:08:02.954
<v ROSAMORE>In back of our houses.

146
00:08:02.954 --> 00:08:04.430
<v ARSENEAULT>Just go in the back?

147
00:08:04.430 --> 00:08:04.726
<v ROSAMORE>Yeah.

148
00:08:04.726 --> 00:08:08.565
My dad used to teach me and my brother how to shoot birds.

149
00:08:08.565 --> 00:08:14.473
And then my daddy and them, and his brother, used to hunt all the time rabbits and stuff like that.

150
00:08:14.473 --> 00:08:20.675
So they would go in the back of the houses, and they’re hunting rabbits and they come back with the rabbits.

151
00:08:20.675 --> 00:08:21.857
We would shoot birds.

152
00:08:21.857 --> 00:08:25.105
Me and my brother, he taught us how to do that.

153
00:08:25.105 --> 00:08:28.945
And then just all of a sudden, I guess, when [unclear] started buying,

154
00:08:28.945 --> 00:08:34.852
digging the dirt for different things they were doing, then it end up where we had got ponds back there.

155
00:08:34.852 --> 00:08:37.806
And it wasn’t really [closed off?] where nobody could go.

156
00:08:37.806 --> 00:08:41.646
They was just digging for the dirt so they didn’t care what happened.

157
00:08:41.646 --> 00:08:44.599
So we would just go back there, start fishing, too.

158
00:08:44.599 --> 00:08:46.962
<v ARSENEAULT>So the rain water would fill it up?

159
00:08:46.962 --> 00:08:47.553
<v ROSAMORE>Oh, yeah.

160
00:08:47.553 --> 00:08:48.439
Fill it up.

161
00:08:48.439 --> 00:08:50.211
And then the fishes would come.

162
00:08:50.211 --> 00:08:52.278
I mean, the fishes would literally come.

163
00:08:52.278 --> 00:08:56.118
And that’s where we did a lot of fishing when we was young.

164
00:08:56.118 --> 00:08:57.890
Go back there in the holes.

165
00:08:57.890 --> 00:09:02.616
And then my brother and me used to go and swim in the holes, you know?

166
00:09:02.616 --> 00:09:05.274
Now I know that snakes go in holes, too.

167
00:09:05.274 --> 00:09:10.000
But then we was always told that snakes don’t bite in the water. [laughter] So-

168
00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:11.656
<v ARSENEAULT>I don't know if that’s true.

169
00:09:11.656 --> 00:09:13.313
<v ROSAMORE>I don't know if that’s true.

170
00:09:13.313 --> 00:09:15.521
So all the guys would just go fishing.

171
00:09:15.521 --> 00:09:16.625
Go fishing or swimming.

172
00:09:16.625 --> 00:09:19.662
<v ARSENEAULT>So a plant came and dug the holes for the dirt?

173
00:09:19.662 --> 00:09:19.938
Or?

174
00:09:19.938 --> 00:09:21.594
<v ROSAMORE>No, it was a dirt company.

175
00:09:21.594 --> 00:09:22.975
It was called Civil Construction.

176
00:09:22.975 --> 00:09:24.631
Gene [Cow?] and there was Macmanus.

177
00:09:24.631 --> 00:09:27.668
They bought the property from my daddy’s uncle, one of them.

178
00:09:27.668 --> 00:09:29.048
So they were digging holes.

179
00:09:29.048 --> 00:09:31.808
I think they were, I don't know, they were digging.

180
00:09:31.808 --> 00:09:33.741
Taking the dirt and bringing it somewhere.

181
00:09:33.741 --> 00:09:38.986
Well, and then by them digging the dirt and bringing it somewhere, well that made a big old hole.

182
00:09:38.986 --> 00:09:42.575
So as it kept raining and raining and raining, it built up water.

183
00:09:42.575 --> 00:09:47.544
And you know, and then next thing you know, we was able to go back there and fish.

184
00:09:47.544 --> 00:09:49.200
And nobody put fish in it.

185
00:09:49.200 --> 00:09:52.513
It was like the sky, the water, the rain, I don't know.

186
00:09:52.513 --> 00:09:53.617
But it was fish.

187
00:09:53.617 --> 00:09:57.206
<v ARSENEAULT>What would you all do, your dad would like skin them and stuff?

188
00:09:57.206 --> 00:09:58.586
Or what would y’all do?

189
00:09:58.586 --> 00:10:00.794
<v ROSAMORE>Oh, he taught us how to clean fish.

190
00:10:00.794 --> 00:10:07.420
He said, “Because if you ever get to where you know, you all want to go fishing when you get older with your kids,

191
00:10:07.420 --> 00:10:13.493
you might as well clean your fish while you’re already out there on the river instead of waiting to take it home.

192
00:10:13.493 --> 00:10:16.806
And then I’m going to show you all how to do it.”

193
00:10:16.806 --> 00:10:19.014
And so he taught me and my brother.

194
00:10:19.014 --> 00:10:21.223
And we both know how to clean fish.

195
00:10:21.223 --> 00:10:22.327
We both still fish.

196
00:10:22.327 --> 00:10:23.983
And there’s a place I bought.

197
00:10:23.983 --> 00:10:26.192
I bought another place with another fishing pond.

198
00:10:26.192 --> 00:10:27.848
Because I had one at home,

199
00:10:27.848 --> 00:10:33.645
and so my brother comes and we go drive to the back of the house, to the property, and go fishing.

200
00:10:33.645 --> 00:10:40.823
And we feel like eating, you know, we’ll keep them and we’ll clean them and then I’ll fry them in my little old cabin I got.

201
00:10:40.823 --> 00:10:43.307
And we just make it [out of a day?].

202
00:10:43.307 --> 00:10:44.411
But we always did.

203
00:10:44.411 --> 00:10:48.000
I mean, we grew up, me and my brother, on the river.

204
00:10:48.000 --> 00:10:48.324
<v ARSENEAULT>Right.

205
00:10:48.324 --> 00:10:52.213
<v ROSAMORE>Because my dad and mom, we used to take her every Saturday.

206
00:10:52.213 --> 00:10:55.130
So me and my brother would go with them.

207
00:10:55.130 --> 00:11:00.639
So we pretty much learned a lot about fishing by my grandma and my daddy, you know?

208
00:11:00.639 --> 00:11:05.176
So I guess whatever you grew up doing, that’s what you carry with you.

209
00:11:05.176 --> 00:11:07.120
And I did that for years.

210
00:11:07.120 --> 00:11:12.306
And when I started having children, I would bring my kids with me on the river.

211
00:11:12.306 --> 00:11:15.222
We would make a whole day out of it.

212
00:11:15.222 --> 00:11:18.139
I would bring sandwiches and drinks while I fished.

213
00:11:18.139 --> 00:11:21.056
And a few of my kids love to fish.

214
00:11:21.056 --> 00:11:21.704
My boys.

215
00:11:21.704 --> 00:11:23.000
Not my girls.

216
00:11:23.000 --> 00:11:24.113
<v ARSENEAULT>Not so much.

217
00:11:24.113 --> 00:11:26.340
<v ROSAMORE>It was [unclear] in the car.

218
00:11:26.340 --> 00:11:26.711
So.

219
00:11:26.711 --> 00:11:27.824
<v ARSENEAULT>They missed out.

220
00:11:27.824 --> 00:11:29.680
<v ROSAMORE>So they didn’t really care.

221
00:11:29.680 --> 00:11:35.988
But then my boys really, which I’m glad, you know, they kind of picked up the habit.

222
00:11:35.988 --> 00:11:44.523
Because when I go out there fishing now, my little baby boy, well he’s not a baby boy, but he’s twenty-eight years old.

223
00:11:44.523 --> 00:11:49.348
But he’ll go out there with me and we spend the whole day.

224
00:11:49.348 --> 00:11:51.945
And we just love it, you know.

225
00:11:51.945 --> 00:11:52.688
It’s just—

226
00:11:52.688 --> 00:11:53.430
<v ARSENEAULT>It’s relaxing.

227
00:11:53.430 --> 00:11:57.883
<v ROSAMORE>It’s a relaxing thing that would take your mind off of whatever.

228
00:11:57.883 --> 00:12:06.047
Because I was always told if you go and sit by the water any worries you have will go into that water.

229
00:12:06.047 --> 00:12:07.902
It’s like a peaceful day.

230
00:12:07.902 --> 00:12:09.758
And that really is true.

231
00:12:09.758 --> 00:12:17.180
Because when I get stressed or just tired of being around everybody, I just go out there in my pond.

232
00:12:17.180 --> 00:12:19.777
And don’t have to catch the fish.

233
00:12:19.777 --> 00:12:22.375
I just like seeing the water move.

234
00:12:22.375 --> 00:12:24.230
And the quietness, you know?

235
00:12:24.230 --> 00:12:26.457
And even back then, the birds.

236
00:12:26.457 --> 00:12:31.652
Nature would be so, where you could hear it all the time, you know?

237
00:12:31.652 --> 00:12:33.508
And it was like, wow.

238
00:12:33.508 --> 00:12:34.250
You know?

239
00:12:34.250 --> 00:12:35.734
<v ARSENEAULT>Now it’s like more—

240
00:12:35.734 --> 00:12:42.414
<v ROSAMORE>Well where I’m at now is more, I can hear them like I did when I was young.

241
00:12:42.414 --> 00:12:45.012
Because I live in the country now.

242
00:12:45.012 --> 00:12:52.062
But before I moved to Mossville, it’s like it wasn’t as much crickets you can hear no more, owls.

243
00:12:52.062 --> 00:12:55.402
Because they cleared out almost all the woods around.

244
00:12:55.402 --> 00:12:58.000
So that was pretty much gone.

245
00:12:58.000 --> 00:13:02.095
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you all keep hunting after they like started clearing everything out?

246
00:13:02.095 --> 00:13:06.189
<v ROSAMORE>Well, we had other places we can go that was still country.

247
00:13:06.189 --> 00:13:07.554
That wasn’t dolled up.

248
00:13:07.554 --> 00:13:09.260
But the woods cleared out.

249
00:13:09.260 --> 00:13:14.720
Like in Carlyss, because me and my children’s dad, his family were not originally from Mossville.

250
00:13:14.720 --> 00:13:16.084
But they moved there.

251
00:13:16.084 --> 00:13:20.179
His Mama said, whenever he was a baby they moved to Mossville.

252
00:13:20.179 --> 00:13:25.639
So we all kind of, well, he grew up at the latter part of, you know,

253
00:13:25.639 --> 00:13:33.486
kind of like when we was in our, he born in ’58, I born in, he born in ’59, I born in ’60.

254
00:13:33.486 --> 00:13:36.557
So it’s kind of like we grew up together.

255
00:13:36.557 --> 00:13:39.970
So whenever we got older, we end up getting married.

256
00:13:39.970 --> 00:13:43.723
And so me, and him, and my brother would go fishing.

257
00:13:43.723 --> 00:13:48.159
A lot of places you can go other than Mossville by that time.

258
00:13:48.159 --> 00:13:56.689
We had Hackberry, they had Cameron, and the hunting, you can go to Carlyss, you can go to all these places and still could hunt.

259
00:13:56.689 --> 00:13:58.395
They were still country areas.

260
00:13:58.395 --> 00:14:01.125
<v ARSENEAULT>You just had to travel a little further.

261
00:14:01.125 --> 00:14:03.514
<v ROSAMORE>You had to travel a little further.

262
00:14:03.514 --> 00:14:11.703
But you know, back in the day, even when I was with my children’s father, because he would go hunting for coons and rabbits.

263
00:14:11.703 --> 00:14:15.797
And basically that’s how we made our money to pay our bills.

264
00:14:15.797 --> 00:14:18.186
Because we would go to these people

265
00:14:18.186 --> 00:14:19.551
<c.p>they called the trappers.

266
00:14:19.551 --> 00:14:24.328
They would buy the hides from you. [. . .?] even dried them, might have stretched them.

267
00:14:24.328 --> 00:14:27.740
That’s how we paid our bills a lot of times.

268
00:14:27.740 --> 00:14:30.128
<v ARSENEAULT>So they would buy the animal hides?

269
00:14:30.128 --> 00:14:30.811
<v ROSAMORE>Mm hmm.

270
00:14:30.811 --> 00:14:33.199
They had to be certain ones now.

271
00:14:33.199 --> 00:14:39.000
They had to be the rabbits, the raccoons, because they could make hats and all this.

272
00:14:39.000 --> 00:14:41.939
<v ARSENEAULT>How much do you get for a raccoon hide?

273
00:14:41.939 --> 00:14:44.551
<v ROSAMORE>Oh, God, it was pretty money back then.

274
00:14:44.551 --> 00:14:48.796
And if you had like, and he would kill a lot of raccoons.

275
00:14:48.796 --> 00:14:49.449
You know.

276
00:14:49.449 --> 00:14:52.388
But he would eat the raccoons, but I wouldn’t.

277
00:14:52.388 --> 00:14:53.041
But anyway.

278
00:14:53.041 --> 00:14:54.673
<v ARSENEAULT>How do you cook raccoon?

279
00:14:54.673 --> 00:14:55.980
<v ROSAMORE>Well, in an oven.

280
00:14:55.980 --> 00:14:56.306
<v ARSENEAULT>Really?

281
00:14:56.306 --> 00:14:57.612
I never knew that.

282
00:14:57.612 --> 00:14:57.939
<v ROSAMORE>Yeah.

283
00:14:57.939 --> 00:15:00.224
That’s what he used to, bake it.

284
00:15:00.224 --> 00:15:01.204
<v ARSENEAULT>I’ve had rabbit.

285
00:15:01.204 --> 00:15:02.510
I’ve never had raccoon.

286
00:15:02.510 --> 00:15:04.469
<v ROSAMORE>Well, bake it in the oven.

287
00:15:04.469 --> 00:15:07.082
And you could have sweet potato with it.

288
00:15:07.082 --> 00:15:10.020
I mean, to me it looks like a dog.

289
00:15:10.020 --> 00:15:11.653
So I couldn’t eat it.

290
00:15:11.653 --> 00:15:14.918
But anyway, [laughs] but back then, that was the meal.

291
00:15:14.918 --> 00:15:16.224
Everybody would eat it.

292
00:15:16.224 --> 00:15:21.449
Go coon hunting and they would have a big old feast, call all their friends over.

293
00:15:21.449 --> 00:15:22.755
Sitting out there drinking.

294
00:15:22.755 --> 00:15:24.714
Putting the coon on the pit.

295
00:15:24.714 --> 00:15:25.367
Oh, lord.

296
00:15:25.367 --> 00:15:27.000
But anyway, I just—

297
00:15:27.000 --> 00:15:28.956
<v ARSENEAULT>How many people does one raccoon feed?

298
00:15:28.956 --> 00:15:29.515
<v ROSAMORE>Oh, honey!

299
00:15:29.515 --> 00:15:31.750
It could feed about three or four people.

300
00:15:31.750 --> 00:15:33.706
<v ARSENEAULT>I guess it’s bigger than a chicken.

301
00:15:33.706 --> 00:15:34.265
<v ROSAMORE>Mm hmm.

302
00:15:34.265 --> 00:15:36.500
It could feed about three or four people.

303
00:15:36.500 --> 00:15:36.779
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

304
00:15:36.779 --> 00:15:38.176
I never thought about that.

305
00:15:38.176 --> 00:15:38.735
That’s interesting.

306
00:15:38.735 --> 00:15:39.294
<v ROSAMORE>Mm hmm.

307
00:15:39.294 --> 00:15:43.206
And they put the barbecues sauce on it as like a piece of chicken.

308
00:15:43.206 --> 00:15:43.485
Yeah.

309
00:15:43.485 --> 00:15:46.000
But I never had the stomach for it.

310
00:15:46.000 --> 00:15:47.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Never tried it?

311
00:15:47.000 --> 00:15:47.333
<v ROSAMORE>No.

312
00:15:47.333 --> 00:15:50.333
<v ARSENEAULT>It’s really interesting. [you didn’t want to taste it?]

313
00:15:50.333 --> 00:15:55.333
<v ROSAMORE>I didn’t even like the way it smelled when it was baking in the oven.

314
00:15:55.333 --> 00:15:57.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What did it smell like?

315
00:15:57.000 --> 00:15:58.000
<v ROSAMORE>Oh my god.

316
00:15:58.000 --> 00:15:58.333
Funky.

317
00:15:58.333 --> 00:15:58.667
Musky.

318
00:15:58.667 --> 00:16:00.000
Like a weird smell.

319
00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:02.000
But the old hands loved it.

320
00:16:02.000 --> 00:16:09.333
There was a man living around the corner from us, poor thing, he’s dead and gone, but that was my daddy’s cousin.

321
00:16:09.333 --> 00:16:11.000
That’s what he would do.

322
00:16:11.000 --> 00:16:12.667
He would even eat possums.

323
00:16:12.667 --> 00:16:13.333
And armadillos.

324
00:16:13.333 --> 00:16:17.000
My grandma on my mama’s side, she used to eat turtles.

325
00:16:17.000 --> 00:16:20.667
And I couldn’t do turtles, because they like snakes to me.

326
00:16:20.667 --> 00:16:22.000
I couldn’t do turtles.

327
00:16:22.000 --> 00:16:23.000
But anyway.

328
00:16:23.000 --> 00:16:24.669
<v ARSENEAULT>How do you cook a turtle?

329
00:16:24.669 --> 00:16:25.781
Like I’m so curious.

330
00:16:25.781 --> 00:16:27.450
<v ROSAMORE>Well, they make a turtle sauce.

331
00:16:27.450 --> 00:16:28.006
Sauce piquant.

332
00:16:28.006 --> 00:16:30.788
They do egg gravy and bell peppers and all that.

333
00:16:30.788 --> 00:16:32.178
<v ARSENEAULT>And put it over rice?

334
00:16:32.178 --> 00:16:32.734
<v ROSAMORE>Mm hmm.

335
00:16:32.734 --> 00:16:34.681
Definitely. [Grandma?] would just eat the legs.

336
00:16:34.681 --> 00:16:39.409
Well you know, once you take the shell out, that’s not too much to that turtle there.

337
00:16:39.409 --> 00:16:40.800
So you got the legs.

338
00:16:40.800 --> 00:16:41.634
And the skin.

339
00:16:41.634 --> 00:16:43.303
But they do eat eggs, too.

340
00:16:43.303 --> 00:16:46.362
I used to eat the little orange eggs of the turtle.

341
00:16:46.362 --> 00:16:47.197
I didn’t know.

342
00:16:47.197 --> 00:16:49.978
But they sure was good when they was cooked, though.

343
00:16:49.978 --> 00:16:52.481
But I couldn’t do the turtle itself because I

344
00:16:52.481 --> 00:16:58.878
<c.p>mean, you’ve got to like what you’re eating, and when you look at it, it’s like, [makes grimacing sound] I couldn’t do it.

345
00:16:58.878 --> 00:17:02.772
But you know, things were, I mean, you could see the times change drastically,

346
00:17:02.772 --> 00:17:06.388
you know, because we went from being able to do whatever we wanted,

347
00:17:06.388 --> 00:17:12.784
our kids could ride the bicycles on the street, didn’t have to worry about it because cars were coming every now and then.

348
00:17:12.784 --> 00:17:15.844
Then all of a sudden, we had to watch the kids.

349
00:17:15.844 --> 00:17:17.234
We used to [eat nettle?].

350
00:17:17.234 --> 00:17:19.738
So like we had to go to grocery store.

351
00:17:19.738 --> 00:17:21.406
We could leave our house unlocked.

352
00:17:21.406 --> 00:17:23.075
And our doors closed but unlocked.

353
00:17:23.075 --> 00:17:25.578
Stay gone and visit whoever we wanted to visit.

354
00:17:25.578 --> 00:17:26.134
Come back.

355
00:17:26.134 --> 00:17:32.809
And you didn’t have to worry about nobody going in your house because back then everybody was neighbors, so everybody would watch everybody’s house.

356
00:17:32.809 --> 00:17:35.869
Oh, look, I’m going to be going to so and so.

357
00:17:35.869 --> 00:17:37.259
And you tell your neighbor.

358
00:17:37.259 --> 00:17:40.319
Well your neighbor going to watch your house while you’re gone.

359
00:17:40.319 --> 00:17:42.822
And it would go like vice versa, you know?

360
00:17:42.822 --> 00:17:45.603
So it’s kind of like, it was sort of different.

361
00:17:45.603 --> 00:17:52.000
And as the years went, well, then you better lock your house or you come back, all your stuff will be gone!

362
00:17:52.000 --> 00:17:55.326
<v ARSENEAULT>Where did you all go to the grocery store?

363
00:17:55.326 --> 00:17:57.543
<v ROSAMORE>Well, then it was like Westlake.

364
00:17:57.543 --> 00:18:01.978
Because we could go shop in Westlake and go shop in [Sulphur?].

365
00:18:01.978 --> 00:18:04.565
But it was still close to us.

366
00:18:04.565 --> 00:18:07.152
<v ARSENEAULT>Was there a grocery store in Mossville?

367
00:18:07.152 --> 00:18:07.891
<v ROSAMORE>Never was.

368
00:18:07.891 --> 00:18:09.000
Never was.

369
00:18:09.000 --> 00:18:11.189
<v ARSENEAULT>Like a gas station or something, maybe?

370
00:18:11.189 --> 00:18:12.753
<v ROSAMORE>They had a gas station.

371
00:18:12.753 --> 00:18:19.322
It was called, well they had an old man opened up a gas station and his name was Walter B. Smith.

372
00:18:19.322 --> 00:18:23.388
But it was like a little, just a gas station and that’s it.

373
00:18:23.388 --> 00:18:29.018
And then we had the other little store that opened up years later that was called Bell’s 7/11.

374
00:18:29.018 --> 00:18:30.269
That was on OST.

375
00:18:30.269 --> 00:18:33.709
So gas and plus it was like a little convenience store.

376
00:18:33.709 --> 00:18:36.211
And I worked there when I was young.

377
00:18:36.211 --> 00:18:41.216
I was like seventeen, somewhere around there, you know, and we finally got a little store.

378
00:18:41.216 --> 00:18:44.656
And then it was a long time before we had water.

379
00:18:44.656 --> 00:18:46.220
They had to do wells.

380
00:18:46.220 --> 00:18:46.846
Water wells.

381
00:18:46.846 --> 00:18:49.661
When I was growing up, we had water wells.

382
00:18:49.661 --> 00:18:51.537
Until I got a certain age.

383
00:18:51.537 --> 00:18:53.101
And then they ran water.

384
00:18:53.101 --> 00:18:59.357
But the water stopped [. . .?] at the house where I was living [before I sold to?] Sasol [energy and chemical company].

385
00:18:59.357 --> 00:19:05.925
That’s where it stopped and my dad and the family that passed me, they had to get their water from DeQuincy.

386
00:19:05.925 --> 00:19:10.930
You know, we always lived in Mossville, but Mossville stopped the water at a certain point.

387
00:19:10.930 --> 00:19:16.247
And then the other ones that lived still in Mossville, they had to get theirs in DeQuincy.

388
00:19:16.247 --> 00:19:20.000
But yeah, there was a point where you had no water.

389
00:19:20.000 --> 00:19:22.673
<v ARSENEAULT>So it was all in the well.

390
00:19:22.673 --> 00:19:23.436
<v ROSAMORE>No bathrooms.

391
00:19:23.436 --> 00:19:24.964
You know, inside bathrooms.

392
00:19:24.964 --> 00:19:27.636
I grew up with an inside bathroom.

393
00:19:27.636 --> 00:19:35.655
But my mom said before we bought, they didn’t have inside bathrooms, they had to make those toilets on the outside.

394
00:19:35.655 --> 00:19:37.564
<v ARSENEAULT>Like an outhouse or something.

395
00:19:37.564 --> 00:19:37.945
<v ROSAMORE>Yeah.

396
00:19:37.945 --> 00:19:38.327
Yeah.

397
00:19:38.327 --> 00:19:41.000
But I don't remember that part.

398
00:19:41.000 --> 00:19:43.159
<v ARSENEAULT>What did they use for toilet paper?

399
00:19:43.159 --> 00:19:46.551
<v ROSAMORE>Well, you know, they got the toilet paper from the store.

400
00:19:46.551 --> 00:19:51.486
And I think they said back in the day they had to use stuff like lime.

401
00:19:51.486 --> 00:19:52.411
<v ARSENEAULT>To dissolve it.

402
00:19:52.411 --> 00:19:56.729
<v ROSAMORE>In that thing, I guess it was to kill all the whatevers, you know.

403
00:19:56.729 --> 00:20:01.664
Because you couldn’t throw it, because you couldn’t, you had no way to throw it away.

404
00:20:01.664 --> 00:20:04.748
So I guess, I guess they figured out a way.

405
00:20:04.748 --> 00:20:07.215
<v ARSENEAULT>I’m always interested like how our ancestors lived.

406
00:20:07.215 --> 00:20:09.991
Like how did they survive without an indoor bathroom?

407
00:20:09.991 --> 00:20:11.533
We take it for granted.

408
00:20:11.533 --> 00:20:13.383
<v ROSAMORE>Well, and they had the outhouse.

409
00:20:13.383 --> 00:20:14.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

410
00:20:14.000 --> 00:20:17.391
<v ROSAMORE>And I just don’t know how did they really do it.

411
00:20:17.391 --> 00:20:24.173
Because I remember when I was little, I remember going to my mama’s neighbor, you know, and they still had the outhouse.

412
00:20:24.173 --> 00:20:25.714
It was an old lady.

413
00:20:25.714 --> 00:20:27.256
It was my daddy’s cousin.

414
00:20:27.256 --> 00:20:29.722
And I would be scared to go in

415
00:20:29.722 --> 00:20:30.647
<c.p>there, you know?

416
00:20:30.647 --> 00:20:33.113
I would cry not to go in there.

417
00:20:33.113 --> 00:20:37.429
Because you look down there, you know, being a little girl, oh my god.

418
00:20:37.429 --> 00:20:38.970
You’re not used to it.

419
00:20:38.970 --> 00:20:41.436
<v ARSENEAULT>When I go camping, that’s how I’m like.

420
00:20:41.436 --> 00:20:43.902
<v ROSAMORE>You don’t know if nothing was in there.

421
00:20:43.902 --> 00:20:44.211
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

422
00:20:44.211 --> 00:20:45.444
Something might bite you.

423
00:20:45.444 --> 00:20:46.677
<v ROSAMORE>A snake or something.

424
00:20:46.677 --> 00:20:49.451
And I would go all the way back home.

425
00:20:49.451 --> 00:20:52.534
I wouldn’t use the bathroom, because I was too scared.

426
00:20:52.534 --> 00:20:55.000
But it’s strange how things have changed.

427
00:20:55.000 --> 00:20:55.333
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

428
00:20:55.333 --> 00:20:57.667
<v ROSAMORE>We had no lights at one point.

429
00:20:57.667 --> 00:21:01.000
We used to use kerosene lamps over the whole house.

430
00:21:01.000 --> 00:21:02.000
I remember that.

431
00:21:02.000 --> 00:21:05.000
And we used to, we had no air conditioning.

432
00:21:05.000 --> 00:21:07.000
We used to use window fans.

433
00:21:07.000 --> 00:21:09.667
And some houses had attic fans, you know?

434
00:21:09.667 --> 00:21:15.000
And once you turned the attic fan on at night, well, the whole house gets cool.

435
00:21:15.000 --> 00:21:20.667
But then when you wake up in the morning, feel like you damp, or your sheets damp.

436
00:21:20.667 --> 00:21:22.000
But you slept good.

437
00:21:22.000 --> 00:21:24.333
<v ARSENEAULT>How long, when did you get light?

438
00:21:24.333 --> 00:21:26.000
<v ROSAMORE>When did we get lights?

439
00:21:26.000 --> 00:21:26.667
<v ARSENEAULT>Mm hmm.

440
00:21:26.667 --> 00:21:28.000
Do you remember?

441
00:21:28.000 --> 00:21:31.231
<v ROSAMORE>I had to be about, I had to be up there.

442
00:21:31.231 --> 00:21:36.812
Well, I could have been a little bit younger, because I remember being able to get dressed with lights.

443
00:21:36.812 --> 00:21:37.694
Going to school.

444
00:21:37.694 --> 00:21:42.688
I had to be about maybe nine or ten when the lights came, that I can remember.

445
00:21:42.688 --> 00:21:45.919
Just anything before that, I don't remember us having lights then.

446
00:21:45.919 --> 00:21:47.681
But we had those kerosene lamps.

447
00:21:47.681 --> 00:21:52.969
<v ARSENEAULT>What if you had to get up in the middle of the night and you had to light—

448
00:21:52.969 --> 00:21:53.556
<v ROSAMORE>Mm hmm.

449
00:21:53.556 --> 00:21:54.144
Pretty much.

450
00:21:54.144 --> 00:21:57.962
Or, well, if I had to get up, I would tell my dad.

451
00:21:57.962 --> 00:22:00.900
And my dad would get up and light the kerosene.

452
00:22:00.900 --> 00:22:05.306
And would hold it while I go to the bathroom, you know what I’m saying?

453
00:22:05.306 --> 00:22:08.831
Because other than that, I didn’t know what to do with that.

454
00:22:08.831 --> 00:22:11.475
And I can’t stand them things to the day.

455
00:22:11.475 --> 00:22:12.650
That ain’t no lighting.

456
00:22:12.650 --> 00:22:15.000
And it had an odor, you know?

457
00:22:15.000 --> 00:22:16.894
<v ARSENEAULT>It’s all musky, the oil—

458
00:22:16.894 --> 00:22:19.167
<v ROSAMORE>Had an odor to it, too.

459
00:22:19.167 --> 00:22:19.545
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

460
00:22:19.545 --> 00:22:20.682
It’s so crazy.

461
00:22:20.682 --> 00:22:24.470
I was curious about the gathering places in the community.

462
00:22:24.470 --> 00:22:26.742
Like where did people get together?

463
00:22:26.742 --> 00:22:30.152
<v ROSAMORE>Well, most of the gathering was at your homes.

464
00:22:30.152 --> 00:22:38.864
It was like, say like if like for Christmas, everybody went to my grandma house, which was where my aunt lives now, and

465
00:22:38.864 --> 00:22:41.515
<c.p>my dad and them grew up at.

466
00:22:41.515 --> 00:22:43.030
Everybody would meet there.

467
00:22:43.030 --> 00:22:46.818
And my grandma would make the homemade cakes, homemade pies.

468
00:22:46.818 --> 00:22:52.500
And my dad and them would be [unclear] and then there would be everybody there.

469
00:22:52.500 --> 00:22:54.394
As far as family gatherings.

470
00:22:54.394 --> 00:22:58.561
Now as the years went, then they built the recreation center.

471
00:22:58.561 --> 00:23:03.106
So then you had to reserve and then everybody would go there.

472
00:23:03.106 --> 00:23:09.545
But we did a lot of our gatherings at, you know, like at my dad’s or mom.

473
00:23:09.545 --> 00:23:12.576
Because she was still living at that time.

474
00:23:12.576 --> 00:23:14.848
Because she died in her seventies.

475
00:23:14.848 --> 00:23:18.258
So every gathering was, everybody would go over there.

476
00:23:18.258 --> 00:23:22.424
And that’s where we went the whole day for every gathering.

477
00:23:22.424 --> 00:23:23.561
All the kids.

478
00:23:23.561 --> 00:23:27.348
You know, because they had like three acres of land.

479
00:23:27.348 --> 00:23:30.000
So all the kids were everywhere.

480
00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:30.565
<v ARSENEAULT>Just playing.

481
00:23:30.565 --> 00:23:31.130
<v ROSAMORE>Mm hmm.

482
00:23:31.130 --> 00:23:33.672
<v ARSENEAULT>So it was like holidays you would go there.

483
00:23:33.672 --> 00:23:39.885
<v ROSAMORE>Holidays, or just if one of my daddy’s brothers or sisters was coming in from town, and everybody knew they was coming,

484
00:23:39.885 --> 00:23:45.534
so then everybody would go to my grandma’s house, and my grandma would bake those cakes and all that stuff.

485
00:23:45.534 --> 00:23:47.794
You know, she would bake all the time.

486
00:23:47.794 --> 00:23:50.336
So looks like that was just something she did.

487
00:23:50.336 --> 00:23:50.901
You know?

488
00:23:50.901 --> 00:23:57.397
But if she knew somebody was coming, and she knew what kind of cake they like or what kind of pie they like,

489
00:23:57.397 --> 00:24:00.786
she would have that pie made, you know, and stuff like that.

490
00:24:00.786 --> 00:24:04.176
And then everybody would be loading up over there at my grandma’s.

491
00:24:04.176 --> 00:24:07.000
And there was a bunch of us back then.

492
00:24:07.000 --> 00:24:08.901
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you all just hang out?

493
00:24:08.901 --> 00:24:12.070
What did you all do when you all were there?

494
00:24:12.070 --> 00:24:13.021
<v ROSAMORE>Well, you know—

495
00:24:13.021 --> 00:24:13.972
<v ARSENEAULT>Just eat and—

496
00:24:13.972 --> 00:24:20.944
<v ROSAMORE>Back in the days, you know, kids like us used to play [hopscotch?] the girls, and boys used to shoot the marbles.

497
00:24:20.944 --> 00:24:23.796
So it was like the boys was doing that,

498
00:24:23.796 --> 00:24:30.451
or the boys was shooting basketball or they was going fishing or they was going swimming in the hole back there.

499
00:24:30.451 --> 00:24:37.423
The girls was mostly around everybody else, because it was, because my mama was like, oh, no, you not leaving this [yard?].

500
00:24:37.423 --> 00:24:40.908
So me and my cousin would play jacks or play hopscotch.

501
00:24:40.908 --> 00:24:42.493
And that’s what we did.

502
00:24:42.493 --> 00:24:45.028
And back then, that was fun, you know?

503
00:24:45.028 --> 00:24:49.782
And I remember, I guess my brother must have got bored with all that stuff.

504
00:24:49.782 --> 00:24:52.000
So he built us a treehouse.

505
00:24:52.000 --> 00:24:53.649
<v ARSENEAULT>Is he your older brother?

506
00:24:53.649 --> 00:24:55.298
<v ROSAMORE>He’s my only brother, yeah.

507
00:24:55.298 --> 00:24:56.287
<v ARSENEAULT>Your only brother.

508
00:24:56.287 --> 00:24:57.936
<v ROSAMORE>But he older than me.

509
00:24:57.936 --> 00:24:59.255
<v ARSENEAULT>He’s older than you.

510
00:24:59.255 --> 00:24:59.585
<v ROSAMORE>Yeah.

511
00:24:59.585 --> 00:25:03.213
And so for some reason, he knew how to build stuff.

512
00:25:03.213 --> 00:25:04.862
So he asked my dad.

513
00:25:04.862 --> 00:25:12.117
And one day he said, “Daddy, if you get me the lumber, I’ll build me and Marie kind of like a clubhouse.”

514
00:25:12.117 --> 00:25:13.766
But it was a treehouse.

515
00:25:13.766 --> 00:25:16.074
It was a house in the trees.

516
00:25:16.074 --> 00:25:19.372
And so my dad went and got him the lumber.

517
00:25:19.372 --> 00:25:22.011
And he built that treehouse with some steps.

518
00:25:22.011 --> 00:25:23.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, cool.

519
00:25:23.000 --> 00:25:27.677
<v ROSAMORE>So when our friends would come over, we would have like a little party up there.

520
00:25:27.677 --> 00:25:33.815
Go to the store, buy some sodas and chips and make ourselves, and our mom would make us sandwiches and stuff.

521
00:25:33.815 --> 00:25:37.615
And we all just stepped out, just acted silly, you know, coming down.

522
00:25:37.615 --> 00:25:42.292
And then my daddy, he had a, because he had these big old two oak trees.

523
00:25:42.292 --> 00:25:46.969
And he put us a big old swing with the big old board come across it.

524
00:25:46.969 --> 00:25:48.723
And we would swing on that.

525
00:25:48.723 --> 00:25:53.108
Girl, it was just, back then, we was young, it was like so much fun.

526
00:25:53.108 --> 00:25:54.569
Compared to now, you know.

527
00:25:54.569 --> 00:25:56.323
Kids now want the video games.

528
00:25:56.323 --> 00:25:58.369
That wasn’t even our option back then.

529
00:25:58.369 --> 00:25:58.662
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

530
00:25:58.662 --> 00:26:01.000
<v ROSAMORE>We didn’t know even what that was.

531
00:26:01.000 --> 00:26:02.357
<v ARSENEAULT>What was Christmas like?

532
00:26:02.357 --> 00:26:03.036
<v ROSAMORE>Oh, god.

533
00:26:03.036 --> 00:26:04.732
Christmas was the best ever.

534
00:26:04.732 --> 00:26:06.768
I mean, being a little girl.

535
00:26:06.768 --> 00:26:09.143
Because Mama bought me this baby doll.

536
00:26:09.143 --> 00:26:12.196
I still remember that baby doll to this day.

537
00:26:12.196 --> 00:26:15.250
And it looked like she was taller than me.

538
00:26:15.250 --> 00:26:19.321
But I can hold her hand and she would walk with me.

539
00:26:19.321 --> 00:26:21.357
And I thought oh my God!

540
00:26:21.357 --> 00:26:23.054
You know, I was little.

541
00:26:23.054 --> 00:26:24.750
I was like so excited.

542
00:26:24.750 --> 00:26:28.143
And that’s the memory I remember is that little doll.

543
00:26:28.143 --> 00:26:31.196
And then I used to love to watch Cinderella.

544
00:26:31.196 --> 00:26:33.571
And Mama bought me a Cinderella watch.

545
00:26:33.571 --> 00:26:39.000
And it’s like that’s the two things I remember that I was just so happy with.

546
00:26:39.000 --> 00:26:39.679
You know?

547
00:26:39.679 --> 00:26:42.732
It’s like Cinderella was real, being a little girl.

548
00:26:42.732 --> 00:26:47.821
And this doll was like she could just walk wherever I wanted her to go.

549
00:26:47.821 --> 00:26:50.196
It was like I was guiding her.

550
00:26:50.196 --> 00:26:51.214
It was just—

551
00:26:51.214 --> 00:26:51.893
<v ARSENEAULT>Good memories.

552
00:26:51.893 --> 00:26:52.232
<v ROSAMORE>Yeah.

553
00:26:52.232 --> 00:26:54.946
You know, I mean, I’m sure there’s more.

554
00:26:54.946 --> 00:26:58.000
But that’s the ones that stuck with me.

555
00:26:58.000 --> 00:26:59.508
<v ARSENEAULT>That stick out to me.

556
00:26:59.508 --> 00:27:04.332
<v ROSAMORE>And when my kids ask me, “Mom, how was your Christmas when you were growing up?”

557
00:27:04.332 --> 00:27:07.649
And that’s the only two things I can always tell them.

558
00:27:07.649 --> 00:27:09.760
But I say, I know there’s more.

559
00:27:09.760 --> 00:27:12.173
But that’s what stuck with me, you know?

560
00:27:12.173 --> 00:27:16.997
And my dad, they didn’t give us what we wanted; they give us what we need.

561
00:27:16.997 --> 00:27:18.505
You know what I’m saying?

562
00:27:18.505 --> 00:27:21.822
Because I’m sure I would, I wanted a lot of stuff.

563
00:27:21.822 --> 00:27:23.330
But I didn’t get it.

564
00:27:23.330 --> 00:27:26.647
But then, they made sure that we didn’t go without Christmas.

565
00:27:26.647 --> 00:27:28.155
You know what I’m saying?

566
00:27:28.155 --> 00:27:30.265
And it was hard back then, too.

567
00:27:30.265 --> 00:27:32.979
Because my dad used to work at the plants.

568
00:27:32.979 --> 00:27:37.201
You know, sometimes the plants decide when they want to go on strike and

569
00:27:37.201 --> 00:27:38.709
<c.p>he ain’t got no job.

570
00:27:38.709 --> 00:27:40.518
But he was a good provider.

571
00:27:40.518 --> 00:27:44.740
He knew how to save just in case of things like that, you know?

572
00:27:44.740 --> 00:27:46.851
So we never really went without Christmas.

573
00:27:46.851 --> 00:27:48.660
We never went without our birthdays.

574
00:27:48.660 --> 00:27:50.469
We never went without holidays, period.

575
00:27:50.469 --> 00:27:51.374
So, you know.

576
00:27:51.374 --> 00:27:54.992
<v ARSENEAULT>Did your mom cook for Christmas and all that kind of stuff?

577
00:27:54.992 --> 00:27:56.198
<v ROSAMORE>My mom would cook.

578
00:27:56.198 --> 00:28:03.134
And then when I got older, well, see, back in them days, it was my mom and her mom was living with us.

579
00:28:03.134 --> 00:28:04.340
So they would cook.

580
00:28:04.340 --> 00:28:07.959
And as we girls got older, they taught us how to cook.

581
00:28:07.959 --> 00:28:13.688
So when it was a holiday, we all would be in the kitchen, one doing this, one doing that.

582
00:28:13.688 --> 00:28:15.799
Until we all had the meal done.

583
00:28:15.799 --> 00:28:20.624
And the meal would, we’d just sit back and wait for the meal to be cooked.

584
00:28:20.624 --> 00:28:23.036
You know, about like what they do now.

585
00:28:23.036 --> 00:28:28.162
It didn’t change that much. [laughter]You know, so I mean, I learnt how to cook from them.

586
00:28:28.162 --> 00:28:32.082
And so when my girls got older, I taught them how to cook.

587
00:28:32.082 --> 00:28:33.590
I taught my boys, too.

588
00:28:33.590 --> 00:28:38.716
Because I said because one day, you never know, you might get married or you might not.

589
00:28:38.716 --> 00:28:43.240
And you might get married and then decide that you’re not going to stay together,

590
00:28:43.240 --> 00:28:49.271
and you’ve got to be able to take care of yourself and you should know how to cook for yourself.

591
00:28:49.271 --> 00:28:51.080
And so that’s what I did.

592
00:28:51.080 --> 00:28:53.794
I only taught them what was taught to me.

593
00:28:53.794 --> 00:28:55.000
So, you know.

594
00:28:55.000 --> 00:28:57.246
<v ARSENEAULT>What kind of things would they cook?

595
00:28:57.246 --> 00:28:58.529
<v ROSAMORE>Oh, now, you mean?

596
00:28:58.529 --> 00:28:58.850
Or--

597
00:28:58.850 --> 00:28:59.492
<v ARSENEAULT>Back then.

598
00:28:59.492 --> 00:29:01.417
Like your grandmother and your mom.

599
00:29:01.417 --> 00:29:05.909
<v ROSAMORE>Well it was mostly, a lot of them used to love to barbecue outside.

600
00:29:05.909 --> 00:29:08.476
It was like everybody was an outside person.

601
00:29:08.476 --> 00:29:11.684
Or not an outside person, but everything tasted better outside.

602
00:29:11.684 --> 00:29:16.176
Even when we used to go fishing, we used to cook the fish outside.

603
00:29:16.176 --> 00:29:17.781
And the fish tasted different.

604
00:29:17.781 --> 00:29:20.989
We would bread chicken, we would fry the chicken outside.

605
00:29:20.989 --> 00:29:25.481
It’s like the food just didn’t taste the same from in-house to the outside.

606
00:29:25.481 --> 00:29:29.973
And so we did a lot of outside, smoking meat, you know, frying fish.

607
00:29:29.973 --> 00:29:32.540
Making ice cream, homemade ice cream, you know.

608
00:29:32.540 --> 00:29:35.107
And it would be a whole slew of [. . .?].

609
00:29:35.107 --> 00:29:40.882
Then my grandma would bake the cake, so that we could eat the cake with the ice cream.

610
00:29:40.882 --> 00:29:45.695
And it was like everybody would come over, just to make some homemade ice cream.

611
00:29:45.695 --> 00:29:47.299
<v ARSENEAULT>How would they make it?

612
00:29:47.299 --> 00:29:53.075
<v ROSAMORE>You know, we used to have this ice cream maker that you have to like this, turn it.

613
00:29:53.075 --> 00:29:55.000
<v ARSENEAULT>You had to crank it.

614
00:29:55.000 --> 00:29:55.936
<v ROSAMORE>Yeah, crank it.

615
00:29:55.936 --> 00:29:59.057
And you put ice in there with the rock salt.

616
00:29:59.057 --> 00:30:03.427
And you make up the actual recipe for the strawberry ice cream or vanilla.

617
00:30:03.427 --> 00:30:08.420
And then they would pour it in this big old silver thing, I never forget that.

618
00:30:08.420 --> 00:30:11.854
And then as you turn that ice, it’s making ice cream.

619
00:30:11.854 --> 00:30:15.911
That stuff you mixed up, it’s making it harder and harder and harder.

620
00:30:15.911 --> 00:30:18.720
But you got to turn it and turn it.

621
00:30:18.720 --> 00:30:23.401
And you had to put a towel inside there to make sure it stayed cold.

622
00:30:23.401 --> 00:30:25.898
Just kept turning till we got ice cream.

623
00:30:25.898 --> 00:30:28.395
And ice cream lasts a while, you know?

624
00:30:28.395 --> 00:30:29.331
And that’s what

625
00:30:29.331 --> 00:30:30.580
<c.p>everybody, mainly on Sundays.

626
00:30:30.580 --> 00:30:32.452
You know, everybody went to church.

627
00:30:32.452 --> 00:30:36.510
Or the ones that didn’t go to church, that’s what Sundays would be.

628
00:30:36.510 --> 00:30:40.879
We already know we had to go to [. . .?] house, because everybody having ice cream.

629
00:30:40.879 --> 00:30:42.439
Called an ice cream social.

630
00:30:42.439 --> 00:30:44.624
All the grownups [. . .?] cake and ice cream.

631
00:30:44.624 --> 00:30:47.745
But the kids would get ice cream and cake, too.

632
00:30:47.745 --> 00:30:50.554
But it was mainly for the grownups, you know.

633
00:30:50.554 --> 00:30:51.803
<v ARSENEAULT>Was that at church?

634
00:30:51.803 --> 00:30:53.051
<v ROSAMORE>That was at home.

635
00:30:53.051 --> 00:30:53.363
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh.

636
00:30:53.363 --> 00:30:54.611
<v ROSAMORE>That was at home.

637
00:30:54.611 --> 00:30:58.669
Now at churches, you know, used to have family day and all that.

638
00:30:58.669 --> 00:31:01.790
Then they would have ice cream and stuff like that.

639
00:31:01.790 --> 00:31:04.911
Or you know, everybody would bring a dish, you know.

640
00:31:04.911 --> 00:31:07.096
But I was still kind of young.

641
00:31:07.096 --> 00:31:11.153
Because when I got old enough, because I was going to church myself.

642
00:31:11.153 --> 00:31:16.771
So I was remembering that all that was going after I got old enough to go to church.

643
00:31:16.771 --> 00:31:18.955
But I should have been in church.

644
00:31:18.955 --> 00:31:24.573
But you know how kids are, oh, I don’t feel like going, I don’t want to get up.

645
00:31:24.573 --> 00:31:30.815
Well, I was one of them kids. [laughs] Then when I got older, then I said well, I started going.

646
00:31:30.815 --> 00:31:33.000
And I enjoyed it, you know?

647
00:31:33.000 --> 00:31:35.197
<v ARSENEAULT>Where did you all go to church?

648
00:31:35.197 --> 00:31:38.022
<v ROSAMORE>Well I went to church, it was in Sulphur.

649
00:31:38.022 --> 00:31:40.848
It’s called Willow Spring Baptist, when I was young.

650
00:31:40.848 --> 00:31:42.417
And me and my brother.

651
00:31:42.417 --> 00:31:48.067
And I used to sing in the choir and I actually sing solos and all that good stuff.

652
00:31:48.067 --> 00:31:50.578
But I did that in so many years.

653
00:31:50.578 --> 00:31:58.426
But anyway, but you know, Mama said, well, if you all go, if you all get dressed for church, we’ll make sure you get there.

654
00:31:58.426 --> 00:32:02.821
So me and my brother would get up every morning, get ready for church.

655
00:32:02.821 --> 00:32:06.587
That was a way of us getting out of the house, too.

656
00:32:06.587 --> 00:32:14.121
Because school and home and then, you know, and my brother would go with our cousins and shoot basketball after the rec opened up.

657
00:32:14.121 --> 00:32:19.143
They would go and spend all day at the rec, shooting basketball or just doing whatever.

658
00:32:19.143 --> 00:32:25.108
Then they opened up a swimming pool, so then that was another thing we started being able to do.

659
00:32:25.108 --> 00:32:26.677
All the kids, you know.

660
00:32:26.677 --> 00:32:28.874
And you know, opened up a gym.

661
00:32:28.874 --> 00:32:31.386
And then we had this two-story white building.

662
00:32:31.386 --> 00:32:34.211
I never forget, it was called the Masonic Hall.

663
00:32:34.211 --> 00:32:41.430
It’s where all the children our age would have what they called record hops on Friday nights after you get out of school.

664
00:32:41.430 --> 00:32:43.000
And that’s where, well—

665
00:32:43.000 --> 00:32:44.195
<v ARSENEAULT>What’s a record hop?

666
00:32:44.195 --> 00:32:46.287
<v ROSAMORE>My mama let me go one time.

667
00:32:46.287 --> 00:32:48.080
But—It’s kind of like a party.

668
00:32:48.080 --> 00:32:49.574
You know what I’m saying?

669
00:32:49.574 --> 00:32:54.953
It’s a party where all the teenagers can go and you know, chill and dance off the music.

670
00:32:54.953 --> 00:32:57.343
And so they called that the record hop.

671
00:32:57.343 --> 00:32:59.435
That’s where this guy would spin records.

672
00:32:59.435 --> 00:33:00.929
<v ARSENEAULT>Like a DJ was there?

673
00:33:00.929 --> 00:33:01.228
<v ROSAMORE>Yeah.

674
00:33:01.228 --> 00:33:05.710
And it was one of the guys that was in school with us, actually. [laughs]

675
00:33:05.710 --> 00:33:06.009
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh.

676
00:33:06.009 --> 00:33:09.595
<v ROSAMORE>We used to have the little jukeboxes, you know, with a player.

677
00:33:09.595 --> 00:33:12.583
But then you had speakers you could add to it.

678
00:33:12.583 --> 00:33:18.858
So it was just one person kept spinning the records while everybody else danced and then drinking soda pops or whatever.

679
00:33:18.858 --> 00:33:22.444
And they used to have this lady that lived in Bel Air.

680
00:33:22.444 --> 00:33:25.133
And used to run a canteen, she called it.

681
00:33:25.133 --> 00:33:26.627
But it was for kids.

682
00:33:26.627 --> 00:33:28.121
It was for young children.

683
00:33:28.121 --> 00:33:30.213
She used to have where no alcohol.

684
00:33:30.213 --> 00:33:34.396
But she would have where everybody can go and listen to music in there.

685
00:33:34.396 --> 00:33:37.086
Eat ice cream or floats or whatever, you know.

686
00:33:37.086 --> 00:33:38.879
And that’s how she ran it.

687
00:33:38.879 --> 00:33:42.464
So, I mean, we had fun back in the day, you know.

688
00:33:42.464 --> 00:33:44.855
It don’t seem like it to our children.

689
00:33:44.855 --> 00:33:50.234
But I enjoyed it because they kept us out of trouble, a lot of things out of trouble.

690
00:33:50.234 --> 00:33:52.624
Because usually, trouble was easy to get into.

691
00:33:52.624 --> 00:33:58.899
But if your parent did not find things to keep you busy, then you would have been in trouble a lot.

692
00:33:58.899 --> 00:34:06.669
But my mama made sure if we wasn’t on the fishing creek, we was on camping or they’d find stuff for us to do as families.

693
00:34:06.669 --> 00:34:12.645
You know, because everybody would get together and plan, well, let’s have such and such on Saturday, whatever it was.

694
00:34:12.645 --> 00:34:15.036
Everybody knew, all the family would be there.

695
00:34:15.036 --> 00:34:19.817
So sometimes they was cousins we ain’t seen in a while, and they would come in.

696
00:34:19.817 --> 00:34:23.104
So to me, that was exciting, you know what I’m saying?

697
00:34:23.104 --> 00:34:24.000
It’s just—

698
00:34:24.000 --> 00:34:25.850
<v ARSENEAULT>Where would you all go camping?

699
00:34:25.850 --> 00:34:29.241
<v ROSAMORE>Well, I would have to, there’s this place called Houston River.

700
00:34:29.241 --> 00:34:30.783
You probably heard of it.

701
00:34:30.783 --> 00:34:32.016
<v ARSENEAULT>I think I have.

702
00:34:32.016 --> 00:34:38.182
<v ROSAMORE>And they used to have spots where people used to, they used to have this place called the country slab.

703
00:34:38.182 --> 00:34:42.806
It’s where you would go down the little road and you would get right there.

704
00:34:42.806 --> 00:34:46.198
And there’s this big old country slab where everybody would go.

705
00:34:46.198 --> 00:34:49.281
You could park your campers there and everything or whatever.

706
00:34:49.281 --> 00:34:52.672
And you could stay there for as long as you want.

707
00:34:52.672 --> 00:34:55.447
It was right by the river and all that.

708
00:34:55.447 --> 00:34:58.530
That’s something else that had been changed through the years.

709
00:34:58.530 --> 00:35:05.621
And you would pass up out of a church that I used to go to, that was still considered Houston River back there.

710
00:35:05.621 --> 00:35:10.553
And you used to have [kowpans?] that we could rent, or my dad used to rent,

711
00:35:10.553 --> 00:35:16.411
that we could rent out for the weekend and we could stay by the river all weekend, you know?

712
00:35:16.411 --> 00:35:21.036
But as time got older, now, it’s still there but it’s owned by other people.

713
00:35:21.036 --> 00:35:25.660
But it was just, happens back then that my dad and them used to rent.

714
00:35:25.660 --> 00:35:29.360
But now it’s on this post and you can’t go back there.

715
00:35:29.360 --> 00:35:31.209
I mean, it done change, too.

716
00:35:31.209 --> 00:35:34.601
So I guess, you know, through the years, times just, everything.

717
00:35:34.601 --> 00:35:37.067
It’s kind of like this sort of stuff.

718
00:35:37.067 --> 00:35:38.609
Time just changes, you know?

719
00:35:38.609 --> 00:35:38.917
<v ARSENEAULT>Right.

720
00:35:38.917 --> 00:35:39.534
I know.

721
00:35:39.534 --> 00:35:42.000
<v ROSAMORE>It ain’t like it used to be.

722
00:35:42.000 --> 00:35:45.394
<v ARSENEAULT>I was curious about the weddings in Mossville.

723
00:35:45.394 --> 00:35:45.818
<v ROSAMORE>Oh.

724
00:35:45.818 --> 00:35:47.939
<v ARSENEAULT>Were those big social events?

725
00:35:47.939 --> 00:35:50.909
<v ROSAMORE>Well, any particular one you’re talking about?

726
00:35:50.909 --> 00:35:55.152
<v ARSENEAULT>Well, just like, was it a big like social event?

727
00:35:55.152 --> 00:35:59.394
<v ROSAMORE>Well, the only one I remember is mine, actually. [laughs]

728
00:35:59.394 --> 00:36:02.788
<v ARSENEAULT>Well you can just tell me about yours.

729
00:36:02.788 --> 00:36:06.182
<v ROSAMORE>And I don’t mean to be biased, but—

730
00:36:06.182 --> 00:36:07.030
<v ARSENEAULT>Well, yeah.

731
00:36:07.030 --> 00:36:10.000
<v ROSAMORE>That’s the only one I remember.

732
00:36:10.000 --> 00:36:12.625
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you get married at Willow Springs?

733
00:36:12.625 --> 00:36:17.875
<v ROSAMORE>I got married at Willow Springs Baptist, me and my children, my children’s dad.

734
00:36:17.875 --> 00:36:21.250
And we had the reception at the [Mossville?] rec.

735
00:36:21.250 --> 00:36:23.500
<v ARSENEAULT>Is that what people usually did?

736
00:36:23.500 --> 00:36:24.250
<v ROSAMORE>Mm hmm.

737
00:36:24.250 --> 00:36:25.750
Back in the day.

738
00:36:25.750 --> 00:36:26.875
Everybody was there.

739
00:36:26.875 --> 00:36:32.125
When I say everybody, I don’t mean, not just your family, the whole town.

740
00:36:32.125 --> 00:36:34.375
<v ARSENEAULT>How many people, do you think?

741
00:36:34.375 --> 00:36:35.500
<v ROSAMORE>The whole community.

742
00:36:35.500 --> 00:36:35.875
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

743
00:36:35.875 --> 00:36:37.000
<v ROSAMORE>Oh my God.

744
00:36:37.000 --> 00:36:38.500
Because everybody knew everybody.

745
00:36:38.500 --> 00:36:41.875
So it’s kind of like, wow, she’s getting married.

746
00:36:41.875 --> 00:36:47.125
So it was like everybody took that day off to be at the wedding.

747
00:36:47.125 --> 00:36:50.875
And at the church it was like people were standing.

748
00:36:50.875 --> 00:36:56.125
In the back, all the seats were full, all the pulpit was full, everything.

749
00:36:56.125 --> 00:37:01.000
And when we got to the rec, it was packed there, too.

750
00:37:01.000 --> 00:37:03.538
<v ARSENEAULT>How do you feed that many people?

751
00:37:03.538 --> 00:37:05.351
That’s a lot of work.

752
00:37:05.351 --> 00:37:07.164
<v ROSAMORE>Well, let me tell you.

753
00:37:07.164 --> 00:37:16.229
Back in the day whenever I got married, my husband’s dad cooked for all of his family that came in, you know what I’m saying?

754
00:37:16.229 --> 00:37:19.130
And then he also cooked for the reception.

755
00:37:19.130 --> 00:37:21.668
And then my dad did the same.

756
00:37:21.668 --> 00:37:29.645
So it’s kind of like both families came together and they made it a big event and had enough food for everybody.

757
00:37:29.645 --> 00:37:34.721
Because back then you knew, oh, it’s going to be a bunch of people.

758
00:37:34.721 --> 00:37:39.435
A girl, first time getting married, it’s like they looked highly upon that.

759
00:37:39.435 --> 00:37:44.874
So it was just like, they were just like, everybody was pitching in, pitching in.

760
00:37:44.874 --> 00:37:47.050
You know, bringing this, bringing that.

761
00:37:47.050 --> 00:37:50.676
Also it’s kind of like, but then my dad was

762
00:37:50.676 --> 00:37:54.664
<c.p>on the strike that that particular time when I got married.

763
00:37:54.664 --> 00:38:00.466
And my mom’s mom, she bought my, she bought my groom’s cake, she had that made.

764
00:38:00.466 --> 00:38:03.729
My mother’s twin sister, she bought my wedding cake.

765
00:38:03.729 --> 00:38:05.905
My mom bought my wedding dress.

766
00:38:05.905 --> 00:38:10.618
Let me see, and my dad, my dad paid for all my invitations.

767
00:38:10.618 --> 00:38:14.969
Back then, it was a little bit cheaper but it still was—

768
00:38:14.969 --> 00:38:16.420
<v ARSENEAULT>Like still some money.

769
00:38:16.420 --> 00:38:16.782
<v ROSAMORE>Yeah.

770
00:38:16.782 --> 00:38:20.408
But the weddings was always either given at the house.

771
00:38:20.408 --> 00:38:25.122
Because we had some weddings that was given in the people’s yard, too.

772
00:38:25.122 --> 00:38:26.935
You know, it just depend.

773
00:38:26.935 --> 00:38:30.561
Well, matter of fact, my brother, my brother got married.

774
00:38:30.561 --> 00:38:32.737
And they had a yard wedding.

775
00:38:32.737 --> 00:38:36.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Is it because the yards were big enough?

776
00:38:36.000 --> 00:38:39.782
<v ROSAMORE>No, it was just his wife wanted to have a yard wedding.

777
00:38:39.782 --> 00:38:45.770
Which is nice, because they barbecued at the house while you standing up in the wedding party or whatever,

778
00:38:45.770 --> 00:38:48.921
and you smelled the meat smoking on the pit. [laughs]

779
00:38:48.921 --> 00:38:51.127
<v ARSENEAULT>[laughs] Everyone getting hungry, like, “Hurry up!

780
00:38:51.127 --> 00:38:52.073
Say ‘I do!’”

781
00:38:52.073 --> 00:38:52.388
<v ROSAMORE>Yeah.

782
00:38:52.388 --> 00:38:53.964
And it was all nice.

783
00:38:53.964 --> 00:38:58.061
You know, it was like, it all depends on what a person wanted.

784
00:38:58.061 --> 00:39:01.212
My mama wanted me to be married in a church.

785
00:39:01.212 --> 00:39:03.418
And I was already going to church.

786
00:39:03.418 --> 00:39:06.570
So it was like a big old thing in church.

787
00:39:06.570 --> 00:39:12.558
But then my brother’s girlfriend, well her dad and them wanted her to have a home, in the yard.

788
00:39:12.558 --> 00:39:14.448
And they was nice weddings, too.

789
00:39:14.448 --> 00:39:15.079
They all

790
00:39:15.079 --> 00:39:15.394
<c.p>weddings.

791
00:39:15.394 --> 00:39:16.970
So that’s what they did.

792
00:39:16.970 --> 00:39:23.903
You know, they had it all done just like as if they was in a church, but they was in a yard.

793
00:39:23.903 --> 00:39:26.109
And lord, the people was there, too.

794
00:39:26.109 --> 00:39:28.000
The whole town was there.

795
00:39:28.000 --> 00:39:29.848
<v ARSENEAULT>So that was pretty normal?

796
00:39:29.848 --> 00:39:30.587
<v ROSAMORE>Mm hmm.

797
00:39:30.587 --> 00:39:31.696
Pretty well normal.

798
00:39:31.696 --> 00:39:35.023
You know, because anybody got married, everybody was there.

799
00:39:35.023 --> 00:39:37.611
Because the town was just so small.

800
00:39:37.611 --> 00:39:39.089
And everybody knew everybody.

801
00:39:39.089 --> 00:39:40.198
Everybody was related.

802
00:39:40.198 --> 00:39:46.851
Except for the man I married and the woman that my brother married, we weren’t kin to them.

803
00:39:46.851 --> 00:39:49.069
But everybody else we was, so.

804
00:39:49.069 --> 00:39:51.287
The whole town was our people.

805
00:39:51.287 --> 00:39:53.135
So of course they was [. . . ?].

806
00:39:53.135 --> 00:39:59.789
And everybody that did get married either got married to Mount Zion Baptist Church that was in Mossville.

807
00:39:59.789 --> 00:40:02.376
Or they got married at Willow Springs.

808
00:40:02.376 --> 00:40:05.333
Those was only two active churches back then.

809
00:40:05.333 --> 00:40:08.660
But then we had all the other churches come.

810
00:40:08.660 --> 00:40:10.139
Reverend [Clophus’s?] Church now.

811
00:40:10.139 --> 00:40:13.096
Now we got a church in Bel Air.

812
00:40:13.096 --> 00:40:14.205
I mean, Queensboro.

813
00:40:14.205 --> 00:40:16.792
And it’s called Philadelphia God in Christ.

814
00:40:16.792 --> 00:40:20.119
Well, those churches weren’t there whenever we got married.

815
00:40:20.119 --> 00:40:24.185
That one little church, and I forget the name of it.

816
00:40:24.185 --> 00:40:27.512
I think that was House of Prayer back then.

817
00:40:27.512 --> 00:40:31.947
But it was a nondenominational, but we didn’t belong to it, so.

818
00:40:31.947 --> 00:40:38.231
But most of the people that got married back then, oh, it was a big old bash.

819
00:40:38.231 --> 00:40:40.079
I mean, everybody was there.

820
00:40:40.079 --> 00:40:44.515
Even the ones, even if you didn’t invite them, they was there.

821
00:40:44.515 --> 00:40:47.842
So you knew had to cook enough for everybody.

822
00:40:47.842 --> 00:40:53.756
Because you’re not going to let somebody come and then going to try to feed them.

823
00:40:53.756 --> 00:40:56.713
So you made sure you had enough food.

824
00:40:56.713 --> 00:41:01.888
Even if you didn’t use it all, you would split it between the families.

825
00:41:01.888 --> 00:41:05.584
You know, they take some home, you take some home.

826
00:41:05.584 --> 00:41:10.389
So that the food could be distributed out to everybody there was enough.

827
00:41:10.389 --> 00:41:10.759
Yeah.

828
00:41:10.759 --> 00:41:11.498
Pretty much.

829
00:41:11.498 --> 00:41:12.238
Oh, yeah.

830
00:41:12.238 --> 00:41:15.564
My wedding was, it was very nice, I think.

831
00:41:15.564 --> 00:41:16.673
<v ARSENEAULT>It sounds nice.

832
00:41:16.673 --> 00:41:20.000
<v ROSAMORE>The first wedding is always, will always be—

833
00:41:20.000 --> 00:41:20.755
<v ARSENEAULT>Good memory.

834
00:41:20.755 --> 00:41:23.396
<v ROSAMORE>The one you always remember the most.

835
00:41:23.396 --> 00:41:28.302
You can marry fifteen times, but the first will always be the one.

836
00:41:28.302 --> 00:41:29.434
Because you’re young.

837
00:41:29.434 --> 00:41:32.830
You know, you’re young, you’re hyped up, you’re happy.

838
00:41:32.830 --> 00:41:33.585
And anyway.

839
00:41:33.585 --> 00:41:35.849
<v ARSENEAULT>You’re happy about life and stuff.

840
00:41:35.849 --> 00:41:36.226
<v ROSAMORE>Yeah.

841
00:41:36.226 --> 00:41:36.604
Yeah.

842
00:41:36.604 --> 00:41:37.736
<v ARSENEAULT>What about funerals?

843
00:41:37.736 --> 00:41:40.000
Were those the community, too?

844
00:41:40.000 --> 00:41:42.979
<v ROSAMORE>Oh, well, back then, I‘m trying to remember.

845
00:41:42.979 --> 00:41:48.191
Well, my grandma with my dad and mom, she got married, Willow Springs Baptist.

846
00:41:48.191 --> 00:41:51.543
And my grandpa was gone way before I born.

847
00:41:51.543 --> 00:41:53.404
He died in the ‘40s.

848
00:41:53.404 --> 00:41:57.500
But they say he was buried at Mount Zion Baptist Church.

849
00:41:57.500 --> 00:42:04.947
And the funerals, well, when I was a little girl, I remember they used to have funerals in the homes.

850
00:42:04.947 --> 00:42:07.926
You know, with a casket in the house.

851
00:42:07.926 --> 00:42:10.904
And you had to sleep there all night.

852
00:42:10.904 --> 00:42:13.883
And I used to be scared, scared, scared.

853
00:42:13.883 --> 00:42:19.096
But then when I got older, they started having the funerals at the churches.

854
00:42:19.096 --> 00:42:23.564
And by then, there was only a few, far and between churches.

855
00:42:23.564 --> 00:42:25.798
So but back then, even if

856
00:42:25.798 --> 00:42:31.383
<c.p>you didn’t belong to a church, they would still pass your body through the church.

857
00:42:31.383 --> 00:42:33.245
You know what I’m saying?

858
00:42:33.245 --> 00:42:35.106
But now it’s totally different.

859
00:42:35.106 --> 00:42:38.085
Now you got to belong to a church.

860
00:42:38.085 --> 00:42:42.181
Or somebody that’s related to you is going to that church.

861
00:42:42.181 --> 00:42:48.511
That’s the only way you might get your body through that church if something happen to you.

862
00:42:48.511 --> 00:42:54.840
But oh, yeah, the funeral were always, it used to be where they were in the home,

863
00:42:54.840 --> 00:43:03.032
and then they just somewhere in there, they got rid of that and then they started bringing them to the funeral homes.

864
00:43:03.032 --> 00:43:06.383
Or in the funeral home, you could have there.

865
00:43:06.383 --> 00:43:08.989
But it was mostly at the churches.

866
00:43:08.989 --> 00:43:12.340
But the churches, the only one was in Mossville.

867
00:43:12.340 --> 00:43:16.809
But not everybody wanted to, not everybody was funeralized at that church.

868
00:43:16.809 --> 00:43:20.160
Because some people went to church across the road.

869
00:43:20.160 --> 00:43:24.255
Willow Springs, though, because my dad was funeralized at Willow Spring.

870
00:43:24.255 --> 00:43:25.000
And--

871
00:43:25.000 --> 00:43:27.760
<v ARSENEAULT>Is that where he’s buried— He’s buried there?

872
00:43:27.760 --> 00:43:30.175
<v ROSAMORE>Actually, he is buried at Perkins Cemetery.

873
00:43:30.175 --> 00:43:31.211
<v ARSENEAULT>Where is that?

874
00:43:31.211 --> 00:43:32.246
<v ROSAMORE>That’s in Westlake.

875
00:43:32.246 --> 00:43:32.591
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

876
00:43:32.591 --> 00:43:35.696
<v ROSAMORE>But his mom is there, his brother is there.

877
00:43:35.696 --> 00:43:38.456
A lot of our relatives is in there.

878
00:43:38.456 --> 00:43:42.596
And then a lot of our relatives is in Mount Zion, too.

879
00:43:42.596 --> 00:43:46.047
And then that church, I think it was called, I

880
00:43:46.047 --> 00:43:51.567
<c.p>want to say it was, well, it was called House of Prayer, that I can remember.

881
00:43:51.567 --> 00:43:55.708
Then we got relatives buried back there, too, on my dad’s side.

882
00:43:55.708 --> 00:43:58.468
And it was a church on the corner.

883
00:43:58.468 --> 00:44:07.094
On Prater and OST, and I can’t remember that old church, but they finally tore it down, I guess, because it was infested with bees.

884
00:44:07.094 --> 00:44:09.164
So they finally tore it down.

885
00:44:09.164 --> 00:44:14.684
But I can’t remember the name of the church, because I guess I didn’t go there.

886
00:44:14.684 --> 00:44:16.064
So, I don't know.

887
00:44:16.064 --> 00:44:20.205
At least they have the store, it used to be on Prater.

888
00:44:20.205 --> 00:44:22.620
Kind of like a little country store.

889
00:44:22.620 --> 00:44:24.000
Real country store.

890
00:44:24.000 --> 00:44:26.400
<v ARSENEAULT>What does that mean, a real country store?

891
00:44:26.400 --> 00:44:28.800
<v ROSAMORE>You know like you could buy your bologna.

892
00:44:28.800 --> 00:44:31.500
They can cut it up the size you want.

893
00:44:31.500 --> 00:44:36.900
You know that, ham, you know, but now you got to go and buy your ham in packs.

894
00:44:36.900 --> 00:44:41.400
But when I say country, I mean that you just go there and buy everything.

895
00:44:41.400 --> 00:44:41.700
<v ARSENEAULT>Old-fashioned.

896
00:44:41.700 --> 00:44:42.000
<v ROSAMORE>Yeah.

897
00:44:42.000 --> 00:44:46.800
As [unclear] tells me, there’s a place like that in Eunice and I want to go.

898
00:44:46.800 --> 00:44:47.700
It’s called Slaughterhouse.

899
00:44:47.700 --> 00:44:49.800
Now we used to have a slaughterhouse.

900
00:44:49.800 --> 00:44:55.800
Back then, it used to be called Elkins and Lejay’s, which is going down OST, like you’re going to Sulphur.

901
00:44:55.800 --> 00:45:03.600
And you used to go there and buy your bologna sliced, or your ham or your chicken and your pork chops cooked the way you want.

902
00:45:03.600 --> 00:45:04.800
All that, you know?

903
00:45:04.800 --> 00:45:06.900
That was back in the day, though.

904
00:45:06.900 --> 00:45:09.600
It ain’t been like that in a long time.

905
00:45:09.600 --> 00:45:12.000
<v ARSENEAULT>I wouldn’t even know how to order.

906
00:45:12.000 --> 00:45:18.158
<v ROSAMORE>When I was old enough to remember, I remember this man and this lady used to ride this horse and buggy.

907
00:45:18.158 --> 00:45:21.677
They was the only couple that were doing this horse and buggy.

908
00:45:21.677 --> 00:45:25.489
And his name was Pete Moss and his wife’s name was Ms. Virginia.

909
00:45:25.489 --> 00:45:28.421
They would ride to Elkins and Lejay on their horse.

910
00:45:28.421 --> 00:45:35.459
I guess that’s when it was fading out, when they was the only two, the only couple that still had a horse and buggy.

911
00:45:35.459 --> 00:45:38.098
And, but they had to be in their eighties.

912
00:45:38.098 --> 00:45:42.789
So they didn’t live long after I remember they were there, you know what I’m saying?

913
00:45:42.789 --> 00:45:46.602
They didn’t live long after that, so I guess they was pretty old.

914
00:45:46.602 --> 00:45:47.481
I don't know.

915
00:45:47.481 --> 00:45:47.774
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

916
00:45:47.774 --> 00:45:48.068
<v ROSAMORE>Yeah.

917
00:45:48.068 --> 00:45:51.000
<v ARSENEAULT>I see people still riding their horses around sometimes.

918
00:45:51.000 --> 00:45:51.843
<v ROSAMORE>Oh, yeah.

919
00:45:51.843 --> 00:45:54.373
Because my uncle has a horse.

920
00:45:54.373 --> 00:45:59.854
But it was really plentiful until Sasol bought out a lot of them.

921
00:45:59.854 --> 00:46:04.070
A lot of them had horses that they bought out.

922
00:46:04.070 --> 00:46:04.492
<v ARSENEAULT>Really?

923
00:46:04.492 --> 00:46:05.335
<v ROSAMORE>Mm hmm.

924
00:46:05.335 --> 00:46:06.178
Oh, yeah.

925
00:46:06.178 --> 00:46:09.973
Because Tony, Tony Bernard used to have a rodeo.

926
00:46:09.973 --> 00:46:15.454
It was called Tony’s Arena, where everybody would go there and have rodeos.

927
00:46:15.454 --> 00:46:18.827
And they would walk their horses down Evergreen.

928
00:46:18.827 --> 00:46:21.357
And you know, just exercise them.

929
00:46:21.357 --> 00:46:24.730
Oh, yeah, they had a lot of horses.

930
00:46:24.730 --> 00:46:31.897
But then when they bought out that section, well, that took a lot of horses away, too.

931
00:46:31.897 --> 00:46:32.319
And

932
00:46:32.319 --> 00:46:36.535
<c.p>that pretty much took the country out, too, you know?

933
00:46:36.535 --> 00:46:39.065
Because all that [stands for?] country.

934
00:46:39.065 --> 00:46:43.281
But now, I may not see a lot of horses.

935
00:46:43.281 --> 00:46:47.076
I see squirrels in the yard, rabbits, all that.

936
00:46:47.076 --> 00:46:49.184
It’s a big, major change.

937
00:46:49.184 --> 00:46:50.027
You know?

938
00:46:50.027 --> 00:46:52.135
Still trying to get adjust.

939
00:46:52.135 --> 00:46:56.773
But I guess you [make new memories?] out, like they say.

940
00:46:56.773 --> 00:46:57.616
<v ARSENEAULT>That’s true.

941
00:46:57.616 --> 00:47:02.676
What stories did your elders tell you about the history of Mossville?

942
00:47:02.676 --> 00:47:03.941
<v ROSAMORE>It was gosis.

943
00:47:03.941 --> 00:47:04.362
<v ARSENEAULT>What?

944
00:47:04.362 --> 00:47:04.784
<v ROSAMORE>Gosis.

945
00:47:04.784 --> 00:47:05.205
<v ARSENEAULT>Gosis?

946
00:47:05.205 --> 00:47:06.049
<v ROSAMORE>Mm hmm.

947
00:47:06.049 --> 00:47:07.314
<v ARSENEAULT>What’s that mean?

948
00:47:07.314 --> 00:47:09.000
<v ROSAMORE>Ghosts lived there.

949
00:47:09.000 --> 00:47:09.339
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh.

950
00:47:09.339 --> 00:47:09.678
<v ROSAMORE>Yeah.

951
00:47:09.678 --> 00:47:13.068
My dad used to tell me that all the time.

952
00:47:13.068 --> 00:47:15.102
I mean, I’ve actually experienced it.

953
00:47:15.102 --> 00:47:16.458
<v ARSENEAULT>Where do they live?

954
00:47:16.458 --> 00:47:17.475
<v ROSAMORE>On Evergreen. [laughs]

955
00:47:17.475 --> 00:47:18.831
<v ARSENEAULT>Whose ghost is it?

956
00:47:18.831 --> 00:47:19.847
<v ROSAMORE>We don’t know.

957
00:47:19.847 --> 00:47:21.203
It was just gosis.

958
00:47:21.203 --> 00:47:22.898
You know what I’m saying?

959
00:47:22.898 --> 00:47:30.356
I guess back then, they used to, I don't know, the souls wasn’t resting, is what my dad used to call it.

960
00:47:30.356 --> 00:47:36.797
Oh, yeah, because my dad used to tell me, because he had an auntie that lived off of Evergreen.

961
00:47:36.797 --> 00:47:40.525
But she was, you know, like Evergreen but into the woods.

962
00:47:40.525 --> 00:47:43.576
And he’d say, and she had a picket fence.

963
00:47:43.576 --> 00:47:50.695
And he’d say, and this lady, and she had a son that used to pick at the ghosts all the time.

964
00:47:50.695 --> 00:47:52.051
And he’d say, [unclear].

965
00:47:52.051 --> 00:47:59.508
One night them ghosts come through, and they just ran up and down that picket fence, da, da, da, da, da, da.

966
00:47:59.508 --> 00:48:05.949
And when they got up the hill, [claps hands] knocked him on his face and his whole skin fell.

967
00:48:05.949 --> 00:48:07.305
I said, really, Daddy?

968
00:48:07.305 --> 00:48:09.000
And he say yeah.

969
00:48:09.000 --> 00:48:15.334
I remember when my dad used to walk with me to his mama’s house, which is where my [. . . ?] property is.

970
00:48:15.334 --> 00:48:20.085
And I was a little girl, because I was real little, he used to say,

971
00:48:20.085 --> 00:48:25.785
“I’m going to tell you right now, when the light’s off, you will hear all kind of sounds.

972
00:48:25.785 --> 00:48:29.269
But when I turn the flashlight on, it’s going to stop.”

973
00:48:29.269 --> 00:48:34.020
Well, honey, we walked, there was a sounded like a basketball, boom, boom, boom, boom.

974
00:48:34.020 --> 00:48:38.137
When my dad turned the light on, as long as the light on,

975
00:48:38.137 --> 00:48:40.037
<c.p>we didn’t hear it no more.

976
00:48:40.037 --> 00:48:42.887
The minute he’d turn it off, boom, boom, boom.

977
00:48:42.887 --> 00:48:44.787
And I’m like, oh my God.

978
00:48:44.787 --> 00:48:50.488
And I was walking around, I was walking with him, holding my arm tight up on the hip.

979
00:48:50.488 --> 00:48:57.139
Well we used to go and get some fireworks, and it’s maybe a little [diagonal?], my dad and mom and them,

980
00:48:57.139 --> 00:49:02.839
me and my cousin used to go get fireworks after Christmas and New Year’s and stuff like that.

981
00:49:02.839 --> 00:49:09.807
And honey, you go [. . . ?] as we walked in, something to do was going [chicko, chicko, chicko?], you know, like the branches breaking.

982
00:49:09.807 --> 00:49:12.024
And the minute we stop, it stop.

983
00:49:12.024 --> 00:49:13.291
We go over here.

984
00:49:13.291 --> 00:49:14.241
Chicko, chicko, chicko.

985
00:49:14.241 --> 00:49:19.625
And then we start running. [makes same sound faster] Like it was running right along with us.

986
00:49:19.625 --> 00:49:19.941
Yeah.

987
00:49:19.941 --> 00:49:20.258
Yeah.

988
00:49:20.258 --> 00:49:25.959
And then me, and my cousin, and my mama’s mama was coming from a gathering [. . . ?] of my grandma.

989
00:49:25.959 --> 00:49:28.176
And we was walking down the road.

990
00:49:28.176 --> 00:49:31.659
And all of a sudden, something go up in the air.

991
00:49:31.659 --> 00:49:33.876
Looked like it was a white sheet.

992
00:49:33.876 --> 00:49:36.410
But we really couldn’t tell what it was.

993
00:49:36.410 --> 00:49:41.161
But it was chasing us, coming down on us like it’s [. . . ?] we were just running.

994
00:49:41.161 --> 00:49:45.594
But the time we got in the house, my grandma was out of breath.

995
00:49:45.594 --> 00:49:45.911
Completely.

996
00:49:45.911 --> 00:49:46.544
Oh, yeah.

997
00:49:46.544 --> 00:49:50.662
And there was a tree on Evergreen that was called the sycamore tree.

998
00:49:50.662 --> 00:49:53.512
And everybody knew that that sycamore tree was haunted.

999
00:49:53.512 --> 00:49:56.046
You do not go by the sycamore tree.

1000
00:49:56.046 --> 00:49:58.579
You don’t do nothing by the sycamore tree.

1001
00:49:58.579 --> 00:50:03.013
And this guy decided that we were, I ain’t worried about that sycamore tree.

1002
00:50:03.013 --> 00:50:08.397
I’m going to go there and I’m going to see what that sycamore tree is all about.

1003
00:50:08.397 --> 00:50:13.148
Well he went in there, it was deep in the woods, he went in there.

1004
00:50:13.148 --> 00:50:18.215
When he came out, with them packing him out, he was like totally not even here.

1005
00:50:18.215 --> 00:50:19.798
He was out of it.

1006
00:50:19.798 --> 00:50:20.432
Oh, yeah.

1007
00:50:20.432 --> 00:50:23.915
And I’m going to show you just how bad it was.

1008
00:50:23.915 --> 00:50:27.082
Trick or treating time, we never had trick or treaters.

1009
00:50:27.082 --> 00:50:31.200
Nobody never came down to our house on Evergreen for trick or treat.

1010
00:50:31.200 --> 00:50:35.000
Because it was always told, trick or treat, Evergreen was haunted.

1011
00:50:35.000 --> 00:50:36.149
<v ARSENEAULT>The whole road was?

1012
00:50:36.149 --> 00:50:37.586
<v ROSAMORE>The whole road was haunted.

1013
00:50:37.586 --> 00:50:41.320
And nobody, we didn’t even have to buy candy for trick or treat.

1014
00:50:41.320 --> 00:50:42.470
Because nobody never came.

1015
00:50:42.470 --> 00:50:46.779
They would go on OST all down around there, but they wouldn’t come on Evergreen.

1016
00:50:46.779 --> 00:50:48.215
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you all go out?

1017
00:50:48.215 --> 00:50:48.790
<v ROSAMORE>Mm hmm.

1018
00:50:48.790 --> 00:50:49.939
We would go out.

1019
00:50:49.939 --> 00:50:50.801
<v ARSENEAULT>Were you scared?

1020
00:50:50.801 --> 00:50:51.950
<v ROSAMORE>Oh, no. no. Nighttime?

1021
00:50:51.950 --> 00:50:54.249
No. we would go out in the day.

1022
00:50:54.249 --> 00:50:56.834
Because they didn’t mess with us in the day.

1023
00:50:56.834 --> 00:50:58.271
It was just at night.

1024
00:50:58.271 --> 00:51:02.006
And by night, we would make darn sure we were in the house.

1025
00:51:02.006 --> 00:51:04.591
Oh, honey, you would hear all kind of stuff.

1026
00:51:04.591 --> 00:51:06.602
You would see all kind of stuff.

1027
00:51:06.602 --> 00:51:07.751
Back in the day.

1028
00:51:07.751 --> 00:51:08.326
Oh, God.

1029
00:51:08.326 --> 00:51:10.912
I remember my dad took me and my brother.

1030
00:51:10.912 --> 00:51:13.497
We did fish striking, we used to call that.

1031
00:51:13.497 --> 00:51:15.221
Did you ever hear of that?

1032
00:51:15.221 --> 00:51:15.508
Okay.

1033
00:51:15.508 --> 00:51:17.232
We would go fishing at night.

1034
00:51:17.232 --> 00:51:20.392
And you’d shine a light at the bank of the river.

1035
00:51:20.392 --> 00:51:22.978
And that’s where all the fish, they sleep, right?

1036
00:51:22.978 --> 00:51:26.138
Well they used to have what they called a fish gig.

1037
00:51:26.138 --> 00:51:27.000
<v ARSENEAULT>A what?

1038
00:51:27.000 --> 00:51:33.511
<v ROSAMORE>A fish gig, when you have to, it look like a fork, but it was on a handle.

1039
00:51:33.511 --> 00:51:34.957
And you’d gig them.

1040
00:51:34.957 --> 00:51:35.681
Like that.

1041
00:51:35.681 --> 00:51:36.043
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh.

1042
00:51:36.043 --> 00:51:36.404
Okay.

1043
00:51:36.404 --> 00:51:38.936
<v ROSAMORE>Well, we was heading to the river.

1044
00:51:38.936 --> 00:51:42.915
And my daddy told me and my brother, he said, “Look.

1045
00:51:42.915 --> 00:51:47.617
There’s going to be a man going to come out of the woods.

1046
00:51:47.617 --> 00:51:55.574
And when he pass us up, I want you all to turn around and look and tell me what you all saw.”

1047
00:51:55.574 --> 00:51:59.191
So, and it was nighttime, but the moon was shining.

1048
00:51:59.191 --> 00:52:00.277
I’ll never forget.

1049
00:52:00.277 --> 00:52:02.447
Me and my brother was walking.

1050
00:52:02.447 --> 00:52:07.511
And this man come out of the woods with a grin on, no face.

1051
00:52:07.511 --> 00:52:10.766
Suit on with a white shirt and a tie.

1052
00:52:10.766 --> 00:52:16.553
Had his hands in his pockets and he was whistling. (whistles) And he passed us up.

1053
00:52:16.553 --> 00:52:20.170
And so when he passed us up, I turned around.

1054
00:52:20.170 --> 00:52:21.617
He wasn’t even there.

1055
00:52:21.617 --> 00:52:28.128
Believe me, Mossville, I don't know about all Mossville, but I know Evergreen was called the haunted place.

1056
00:52:28.128 --> 00:52:31.745
And a lot of people back then, it was true.

1057
00:52:31.745 --> 00:52:35.000
Because we wouldn’t have no trick or treaters.

1058
00:52:35.000 --> 00:52:38.705
<v ARSENEAULT>Who do you think it was, the man with no face?

1059
00:52:38.705 --> 00:52:42.074
<v ROSAMORE>I don't know who the man with no face was.

1060
00:52:42.074 --> 00:52:44.095
<v ARSENEAULT>Just some spirit, it wasn’t happy.

1061
00:52:44.095 --> 00:52:46.453
<v ROSAMORE>It was a spirit that wasn’t rested.

1062
00:52:46.453 --> 00:52:52.516
But they say, you know, when you see ghosts, it’s either somebody died that wasn’t ready to go,

1063
00:52:52.516 --> 00:52:55.884
or there’s somebody died and their soul wasn’t at rest.

1064
00:52:55.884 --> 00:52:59.589
<v ARSENEAULT>Was it somebody, maybe, who was killed in Mossville or something?

1065
00:52:59.589 --> 00:53:00.263
<v ROSAMORE>Mm hmm.

1066
00:53:00.263 --> 00:53:00.937
Mm hmm.

1067
00:53:00.937 --> 00:53:02.284
Had to have been.

1068
00:53:02.284 --> 00:53:08.684
Back then, you know, it was a lot of, not going to say killing, but, well, before my time,

1069
00:53:08.684 --> 00:53:14.411
and I think it was before my [unclear] time, a lot of people were killed in Mossville.

1070
00:53:14.411 --> 00:53:14.747
Indians.

1071
00:53:14.747 --> 00:53:15.421
All that.

1072
00:53:15.421 --> 00:53:16.095
You know?

1073
00:53:16.095 --> 00:53:17.442
So the ghosts stayed.

1074
00:53:17.442 --> 00:53:18.789
And it was haunted.

1075
00:53:18.789 --> 00:53:23.842
And you know, that’s the most, I mean, that’s what I considered the ghost area.

1076
00:53:23.842 --> 00:53:28.558
Because the street I grew up on, that was the haunted street in Mossville.

1077
00:53:28.558 --> 00:53:32.937
And nobody would come at nighttime, you wouldn’t see nobody walking down Evergreen.

1078
00:53:32.937 --> 00:53:34.958
You’d see them walking around everywhere.

1079
00:53:34.958 --> 00:53:35.632
Not Evergreen.

1080
00:53:35.632 --> 00:53:39.000
We wouldn’t even walk it at night by ourselves.

1081
00:53:39.000 --> 00:53:42.372
<v ARSENEAULT>Would you go trick or treating in the day or something?

1082
00:53:42.372 --> 00:53:43.905
<v ROSAMORE>No. We would just go—

1083
00:53:43.905 --> 00:53:46.358
<v ARSENEAULT>On the other side of town or something.

1084
00:53:46.358 --> 00:53:48.810
<v ROSAMORE>We would leave Evergreen and go to Queensboro.

1085
00:53:48.810 --> 00:53:50.650
Or down OST into Bel Air.

1086
00:53:50.650 --> 00:53:53.715
We had to go out to go trick or treating.

1087
00:53:53.715 --> 00:53:57.701
Everybody else would be trick or treating on the main drag, you know?

1088
00:53:57.701 --> 00:53:59.234
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you all dress up?

1089
00:53:59.234 --> 00:53:59.540
<v ROSAMORE>Yeah.

1090
00:53:59.540 --> 00:54:00.153
Oh, yeah.

1091
00:54:00.153 --> 00:54:00.766
Oh, yeah.

1092
00:54:00.766 --> 00:54:04.139
Oh, yeah, they were going to make sure of that, honey.

1093
00:54:04.139 --> 00:54:04.752
Oh, yeah.

1094
00:54:04.752 --> 00:54:06.898
It was so much fun back then.

1095
00:54:06.898 --> 00:54:11.803
You know, and like now that you’re getting older, it’s like wow, I miss those days.

1096
00:54:11.803 --> 00:54:12.416
You know?

1097
00:54:12.416 --> 00:54:15.175
And I was telling my daughter the other day,

1098
00:54:15.175 --> 00:54:19.774
<c.p>I said, “You know, I lived in Mossville, when I moved, I was fifty-three years.

1099
00:54:19.774 --> 00:54:21.920
I born there, I grew up there.”

1100
00:54:21.920 --> 00:54:28.971
And I said, “What’s the most saddest part about it all is that they children will never know where I really came from,

1101
00:54:28.971 --> 00:54:31.423
because it won’t be there to tell it.

1102
00:54:31.423 --> 00:54:32.650
Or to show them.”

1103
00:54:32.650 --> 00:54:36.635
You know like when you move sometime and you stay gone for years.

1104
00:54:36.635 --> 00:54:42.460
And you come back and you say, “Look, that’s where my mom lived when she was a little girl.”

1105
00:54:42.460 --> 00:54:44.299
We ain’t going to have that.

1106
00:54:44.299 --> 00:54:44.912
You know?

1107
00:54:44.912 --> 00:54:46.445
You know what I’m saying?

1108
00:54:46.445 --> 00:54:48.285
So, but I soon began again.

1109
00:54:48.285 --> 00:54:51.350
I wouldn’t have wanted to stay there for that, either.

1110
00:54:51.350 --> 00:54:52.577
So I don't know.

1111
00:54:52.577 --> 00:54:54.109
Do I really care now?

1112
00:54:54.109 --> 00:54:58.401
I mean, my grandchildren, the older ones grew up there for so many years.

1113
00:54:58.401 --> 00:55:01.161
Now the younger ones, you know, they won’t remember.

1114
00:55:01.161 --> 00:55:03.000
Because I’ve got fourteen grandchildren.

1115
00:55:03.000 --> 00:55:03.317
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

1116
00:55:03.317 --> 00:55:09.974
<v ROSAMORE>And my three oldest, well, because I got, because my other daughter got children the same age as my oldest daughter.

1117
00:55:09.974 --> 00:55:14.095
So I would say about one, two, three, four, five, six, seven, eight,

1118
00:55:14.095 --> 00:55:19.484
by ten of them we’ll remember a little bit about Mossville, but not that much, you know?

1119
00:55:19.484 --> 00:55:21.386
But the three oldest ones will.

1120
00:55:21.386 --> 00:55:24.556
Because when they left, they were like 14 and 15.

1121
00:55:24.556 --> 00:55:27.725
And I’ve still got pictures of, [gatherings?] that we done.

1122
00:55:27.725 --> 00:55:31.529
In my very first house there and at my second thought there.

1123
00:55:31.529 --> 00:55:35.967
And you know, so, if it’s all about memories, it’s all about keeping photos.

1124
00:55:35.967 --> 00:55:43.575
And me remembering things I’ve done, places I’ve, you know, I’ve been in there hunting and picking blackberries and, you know, stuff like that.

1125
00:55:43.575 --> 00:55:45.477
But they would never remember that.

1126
00:55:45.477 --> 00:55:48.647
But at least I got pictures to show, you know?

1127
00:55:48.647 --> 00:55:50.866
<v ARSENEAULT>There’s a lot of history in Mossville.

1128
00:55:50.866 --> 00:55:51.817
<v ROSAMORE>Oh, a lot.

1129
00:55:51.817 --> 00:55:55.938
<v ARSENEAULT>And we have like a, all the different stories everyone’s been telling us.

1130
00:55:55.938 --> 00:56:00.693
<v ROSAMORE>And see the older ones, older than me, got even more to tell than me.

1131
00:56:00.693 --> 00:56:06.399
See, I’m telling it from what I haven’t experienced, what my daddy used to tell me growing up.

1132
00:56:06.399 --> 00:56:08.618
Because I was a nosy little girl.

1133
00:56:08.618 --> 00:56:09.252
Very nosy.

1134
00:56:09.252 --> 00:56:10.837
I wanted to know everything!

1135
00:56:10.837 --> 00:56:12.105
And he would talk.

1136
00:56:12.105 --> 00:56:15.908
I mean, he would treat me like I was a big girl.

1137
00:56:15.908 --> 00:56:18.761
You know, he would tell me everything I asked.

1138
00:56:18.761 --> 00:56:24.467
And that’s how come I learned a lot, because I always was nosy and I wanted to know.

1139
00:56:24.467 --> 00:56:29.856
But I wish I could have asked more questions as far as where my grandma came from.

1140
00:56:29.856 --> 00:56:33.343
Because everybody kept telling me that she come from up north.

1141
00:56:33.343 --> 00:56:37.147
But they say she’s Indian, but I never could really prove that.

1142
00:56:37.147 --> 00:56:40.000
But that could be back in the time.

1143
00:56:40.000 --> 00:56:41.303
<v ARSENEAULT>Your mom’s mom?

1144
00:56:41.303 --> 00:56:42.171
<v ROSAMORE>Mm hmm.

1145
00:56:42.171 --> 00:56:43.039
My dad.

1146
00:56:43.039 --> 00:56:44.342
<v ARSENEAULT>Your dad’s mom?

1147
00:56:44.342 --> 00:56:45.211
<v ROSAMORE>Mm hmm.

1148
00:56:45.211 --> 00:56:46.947
<v ARSENEAULT>She was a Perkins?

1149
00:56:46.947 --> 00:56:49.118
<v ROSAMORE>Well, she was a Rigmaiden.

1150
00:56:49.118 --> 00:56:49.987
Laura Rigmaiden.

1151
00:56:49.987 --> 00:56:52.592
But her mama was a Perkins.

1152
00:56:52.592 --> 00:56:55.632
And then her daddy was a Rigmaiden.

1153
00:56:55.632 --> 00:57:02.145
But I was told that my grandma’s mom, the Perkins woman, was a full-blooded Cherokee.

1154
00:57:02.145 --> 00:57:09.092
And my grandma, I think I might have gave you all a picture of her, too.

1155
00:57:09.092 --> 00:57:12.132
Well anyway, she looked like an Indian.

1156
00:57:12.132 --> 00:57:13.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

1157
00:57:13.000 --> 00:57:20.156
<v ROSAMORE>But then when you look back into the genealogy stuff, that was Braxton’s on the Cherokee road, too,

1158
00:57:20.156 --> 00:57:28.504
so I don't know if my dad’s dad wasn’t, because they the ones with the high cheekbones was really the Indian?

1159
00:57:28.504 --> 00:57:30.492
<v ARSENEAULT>So your dad’s a Braxton?

1160
00:57:30.492 --> 00:57:31.287
<v ROSAMORE>Mm hmm.

1161
00:57:31.287 --> 00:57:33.672
<v ARSENEAULT>And your mom was a Rigmaiden.

1162
00:57:33.672 --> 00:57:36.057
<v ROSAMORE>No. My mom is from Sulphur.

1163
00:57:36.057 --> 00:57:36.455
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh.

1164
00:57:36.455 --> 00:57:36.852
Okay.

1165
00:57:36.852 --> 00:57:37.648
<v ROSAMORE>His mom.

1166
00:57:37.648 --> 00:57:38.045
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1167
00:57:38.045 --> 00:57:40.033
Sorry I’m trying to like—

1168
00:57:40.033 --> 00:57:42.020
<v ROSAMORE>His dad was Lee Braxton.

1169
00:57:42.020 --> 00:57:44.008
His mama was Aretha Rigmaiden.

1170
00:57:44.008 --> 00:57:46.791
But then her mother was Melissa Perkins.

1171
00:57:46.791 --> 00:57:57.127
And when I went to the Cherokee place in DeRidder, when I called the lady, you know, they usually let you actually look at records there.

1172
00:57:57.127 --> 00:57:59.115
And I called the lady.

1173
00:57:59.115 --> 00:58:05.475
And that lady said, “Well that name, Melissa Perkins, I’ve heard that a lot around here.”

1174
00:58:05.475 --> 00:58:11.041
But I’ve never got the chance to go, you know, go back and see.

1175
00:58:11.041 --> 00:58:15.414
And I’ve got some cousins, the Praters that live on OST.

1176
00:58:15.414 --> 00:58:18.992
Their grandmother was my grandma’s aunt by her mom.

1177
00:58:18.992 --> 00:58:20.582
So there was Perkinses.

1178
00:58:20.582 --> 00:58:20.980
See?

1179
00:58:20.980 --> 00:58:26.943
And they say that there would just be nothing but Indians before anybody else came.

1180
00:58:26.943 --> 00:58:28.930
You know what I’m saying?

1181
00:58:28.930 --> 00:58:33.303
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you know when like the black people started coming in?

1182
00:58:33.303 --> 00:58:41.254
<v ROSAMORE>Well, according to the courthouse, my dad’s great-great-great grandfather, which is Griffin Braxton, came back in 1860, is when he,

1183
00:58:41.254 --> 00:58:50.000
what’s called squatter rights back then, when you could just find your piece of land, and if you keep it up—

1184
00:58:50.000 --> 00:58:52.353
<v ARSENEAULT>You get to keep it?

1185
00:58:52.353 --> 00:58:53.294
<v ROSAMORE>Mm hmm.

1186
00:58:53.294 --> 00:58:53.765
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

1187
00:58:53.765 --> 00:58:57.059
<v ROSAMORE>And he had got like sixty acres,

1188
00:58:57.059 --> 00:59:07.412
like the president signed over sixty acres for him to live on and farm and sell crops, whatever he had to do.

1189
00:59:07.412 --> 00:59:11.647
And so it had to be by the 1860s.

1190
00:59:11.647 --> 00:59:14.471
But he was originally from Virginia.

1191
00:59:14.471 --> 00:59:16.824
Because he born in Virginia.

1192
00:59:16.824 --> 00:59:21.059
Now, my grandma, my grandma’s family, I don't know.

1193
00:59:21.059 --> 00:59:26.235
I found her mother at one point living in Minden, Louisiana.

1194
00:59:26.235 --> 00:59:29.059
And that’s like in Claiborne Parish.

1195
00:59:29.059 --> 00:59:30.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1196
00:59:30.000 --> 00:59:35.091
<v ROSAMORE>And I remember her dad living in, up there toward DeQuincy and DeRidder [Louisiana].

1197
00:59:35.091 --> 00:59:35.455
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1198
00:59:35.455 --> 00:59:40.545
<v ROSAMORE>And that’s where they say, you know, a lot of the Indians come from.

1199
00:59:40.545 --> 00:59:41.636
Up that way.

1200
00:59:41.636 --> 00:59:42.727
I don't know.

1201
00:59:42.727 --> 00:59:43.818
<v ARSENEAULT>So could be.

1202
00:59:43.818 --> 00:59:44.182
<v ROSAMORE>Yeah.

1203
00:59:44.182 --> 00:59:46.727
But I can’t really prove it because—

1204
00:59:46.727 --> 00:59:48.545
<v ARSENEAULT>They don’t keep the records.

1205
00:59:48.545 --> 00:59:55.818
<v ROSAMORE>Well the Indian part, if they never live on the reservation, then you probably never be able to find them.

1206
00:59:55.818 --> 01:00:00.545
Because back in the day, the Indians would never say they were Indians.

1207
01:00:00.545 --> 01:00:02.727
They always say they were black.

1208
01:00:02.727 --> 01:00:04.545
You know what I’m saying?

1209
01:00:04.545 --> 01:00:04.909
<v ARSENEAULT>Why?

1210
01:00:04.909 --> 01:00:13.273
<v ROSAMORE>Like my grandma, even though she was Indian, it was not good for them to say they were Indians back in the day.

1211
01:00:13.273 --> 01:00:18.000
So they always say they were black, but they were really Indians.

1212
01:00:18.000 --> 01:00:18.732
<v ARSENEAULT>Got you.

1213
01:00:18.732 --> 01:00:21.295
<v ROSAMORE>Because things were not good with Indians.

1214
01:00:21.295 --> 01:00:22.027
<v ARSENEAULT>I know.

1215
01:00:22.027 --> 01:00:24.223
The government, they were trying to—

1216
01:00:24.223 --> 01:00:24.589
<v ROSAMORE>Yeah.

1217
01:00:24.589 --> 01:00:27.152
So they wouldn’t say they were Indians.

1218
01:00:27.152 --> 01:00:27.884
<v ARSENEAULT>Got you.

1219
01:00:27.884 --> 01:00:32.643
<v ROSAMORE>But they also had Indian slave owners, too, now don’t get me wrong.

1220
01:00:32.643 --> 01:00:33.375
They did.

1221
01:00:33.375 --> 01:00:34.107
I heard.

1222
01:00:34.107 --> 01:00:35.571
You know, but yeah.

1223
01:00:35.571 --> 01:00:41.062
So she probably is, but we can’t prove it because she could not claim that.

1224
01:00:41.062 --> 01:00:42.527
<v ARSENEAULT>She wouldn’t have said.

1225
01:00:42.527 --> 01:00:44.357
<v ROSAMORE>No. They wouldn’t have said.

1226
01:00:44.357 --> 01:00:46.920
Not unless they was on a reservation.

1227
01:00:46.920 --> 01:00:49.482
It was hard back then, you know?

1228
01:00:49.482 --> 01:00:50.214
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes, indeed.

1229
01:00:50.214 --> 01:00:51.312
What a challenge.

1230
01:00:51.312 --> 01:00:56.804
I was curious about how you all were expected to behave in front of adults.

1231
01:00:56.804 --> 01:00:59.000
<v ROSAMORE>How we were expected to?

1232
01:00:59.000 --> 01:01:00.658
<v ARSENEAULT>Back when you were younger.

1233
01:01:00.658 --> 01:01:00.990
<v ROSAMORE>Oh!

1234
01:01:00.990 --> 01:01:01.653
Oh, honey.

1235
01:01:01.653 --> 01:01:06.960
If my mom had company, she didn't have to say nothing, but give me the eye.

1236
01:01:06.960 --> 01:01:11.271
And I know the eye mean you better get your butt somewhere else.

1237
01:01:11.271 --> 01:01:12.266
<v ARSENEAULT>Just go play?

1238
01:01:12.266 --> 01:01:15.915
<v ROSAMORE>I mean, she didn’t have to say, “Get out of here.”

1239
01:01:15.915 --> 01:01:17.905
No. She just look at you.

1240
01:01:17.905 --> 01:01:20.226
And you already know what that meant.

1241
01:01:20.226 --> 01:01:24.869
Because she would say, “If I give you that look, [unclear] out of there.”

1242
01:01:24.869 --> 01:01:29.844
And if you walk in the room and she got company, no. You already knew.

1243
01:01:29.844 --> 01:01:38.136
Once she give you that look, that means—[whistles] And if you don’t move, she’d tell the company, “Could you excuse me just for one minute?”

1244
01:01:38.136 --> 01:01:41.121
And you already knew what’s going to happen. [laughter]

1245
01:01:41.121 --> 01:01:42.779
<v ARSENEAULT>What was about to happen?

1246
01:01:42.779 --> 01:01:43.774
You would get—

1247
01:01:43.774 --> 01:01:45.764
<v ROSAMORE>Get your butt tore up, honey.

1248
01:01:45.764 --> 01:01:46.759
With a switch.

1249
01:01:46.759 --> 01:01:48.417
She didn’t use a belt.

1250
01:01:48.417 --> 01:01:49.744
A switch hurt worse.

1251
01:01:49.744 --> 01:01:59.030
So she would go and get a switch. [. . .?] go and get a switch and then call you off to the back of the house and whip your butt.

1252
01:01:59.030 --> 01:02:05.000
Then she’d go back in the company, like, “Girl, I’m so sorry.” [laughter] “That girl, I swear.”

1253
01:02:05.000 --> 01:02:07.629
<v ARSENEAULT>“I had to go take care of something.”

1254
01:02:07.629 --> 01:02:10.257
<v ROSAMORE>“I had to go take care of something.

1255
01:02:10.257 --> 01:02:12.886
I told her not to come in here.”

1256
01:02:12.886 --> 01:02:15.514
And just get back like it never happened.

1257
01:02:15.514 --> 01:02:15.843
Yeah.

1258
01:02:15.843 --> 01:02:18.800
<v ARSENEAULT>So your mom was mainly the one who disciplined?

1259
01:02:18.800 --> 01:02:19.457
<v ROSAMORE>Oh, yeah.

1260
01:02:19.457 --> 01:02:20.771
My dad was easygoing.

1261
01:02:20.771 --> 01:02:23.729
He left that up to my mama, I think.

1262
01:02:23.729 --> 01:02:24.386
You know?

1263
01:02:24.386 --> 01:02:29.314
And I didn’t hardly catch no whipping, but she whipped my brother a lot, though.

1264
01:02:29.314 --> 01:02:30.629
For stuff I done.

1265
01:02:30.629 --> 01:02:32.929
Because I tell you, I was spoiled.

1266
01:02:32.929 --> 01:02:34.900
And that was wrong, you know.

1267
01:02:34.900 --> 01:02:36.214
But I was spoiled.

1268
01:02:36.214 --> 01:02:37.529
What can I say?

1269
01:02:37.529 --> 01:02:39.171
I didn’t want the whippings.

1270
01:02:39.171 --> 01:02:40.814
<v ARSENEAULT>You were the only girl.

1271
01:02:40.814 --> 01:02:41.143
<v ROSAMORE>Yeah.

1272
01:02:41.143 --> 01:02:43.114
It was just me and him.

1273
01:02:43.114 --> 01:02:43.443
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1274
01:02:43.443 --> 01:02:45.743
<v ROSAMORE>Yeah, it was just two of us.

1275
01:02:45.743 --> 01:02:46.071
<v ARSENEAULT>Funny.

1276
01:02:46.071 --> 01:02:49.686
<v ROSAMORE>It wasn’t too much, I mean, it was with every parent.

1277
01:02:49.686 --> 01:02:56.257
And if I was at, say, my cousin house and if I do something wrong, that mama can whip me.

1278
01:02:56.257 --> 01:03:01.514
Then when I got home, she would have told my mama, and I’d get another whipping.

1279
01:03:01.514 --> 01:03:02.829
See what I’m saying?

1280
01:03:02.829 --> 01:03:06.443
And if you was at school, the principals could whip you.

1281
01:03:06.443 --> 01:03:10.386
And then when you get home, your mama could whip you again.

1282
01:03:10.386 --> 01:03:14.000
<v ARSENEAULT>When she found out that you were bad in school.

1283
01:03:14.000 --> 01:03:16.931
<v ROSAMORE>But the principal had the right to whip you.

1284
01:03:16.931 --> 01:03:19.863
And when you got home, you got another whipping.

1285
01:03:19.863 --> 01:03:22.469
I mean, that’s how it has changed now.

1286
01:03:22.469 --> 01:03:24.749
Now the principals don’t touch the kids.

1287
01:03:24.749 --> 01:03:27.029
But back in our times, oh, yeah.

1288
01:03:27.029 --> 01:03:31.263
Even the teachers had the right to whip you if you was misbehaving.

1289
01:03:31.263 --> 01:03:33.869
And then they’ll make sure your mama knew.

1290
01:03:33.869 --> 01:03:37.777
And when you got home, you got another whipping from your mama.

1291
01:03:37.777 --> 01:03:42.989
It was like, you know, as far as families, they all looked out for everybody’s kids.

1292
01:03:42.989 --> 01:03:50.480
And if I had your, if I had your child at my house, that was my responsibility to watch her, or watch him.

1293
01:03:50.480 --> 01:03:56.669
So if he did wrong, I’m going to whip him just like I did, just like I’ll do mine.

1294
01:03:56.669 --> 01:03:57.320
You know?

1295
01:03:57.320 --> 01:03:58.949
And the parents had nothing—

1296
01:03:58.949 --> 01:04:00.577
<v ARSENEAULT>They weren’t upset about it?

1297
01:04:00.577 --> 01:04:02.206
<v ROSAMORE>They was okay with that.

1298
01:04:02.206 --> 01:04:07.091
Because they rather you whip my child than they go out there and get killed.

1299
01:04:07.091 --> 01:04:10.349
Because, you know, and you was afraid to whip them.

1300
01:04:10.349 --> 01:04:11.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Right.

1301
01:04:11.000 --> 01:04:12.814
<v ROSAMORE>So that’s how it was.

1302
01:04:12.814 --> 01:04:15.354
We always, I mean, they whipped us.

1303
01:04:15.354 --> 01:04:15.717
Neighbors.

1304
01:04:15.717 --> 01:04:18.619
They didn’t have to be a family member.

1305
01:04:18.619 --> 01:04:22.973
If that lady come back and tell my mama, “Well, you know,

1306
01:04:22.973 --> 01:04:30.593
I tried to tell your daughter not to go over here, go over there, she went anyway, and I whipped her.”

1307
01:04:30.593 --> 01:04:33.133
That’s all my mama had to hear.

1308
01:04:33.133 --> 01:04:34.947
She didn’t want nothing else.

1309
01:04:34.947 --> 01:04:36.761
Don’t tell me nothing else.

1310
01:04:36.761 --> 01:04:38.575
You whipped her, that’s good.

1311
01:04:38.575 --> 01:04:41.115
Now she’s going to get another whipping.

1312
01:04:41.115 --> 01:04:42.566
Caught another whipping, too.

1313
01:04:42.566 --> 01:04:46.920
So you learn when you go to somebody’s house, you better behave.

1314
01:04:46.920 --> 01:04:50.186
Or you’ll get twice the whippings. [laughs] It’s crazy.

1315
01:04:50.186 --> 01:04:50.912
<v ARSENEAULT>That’s funny.

1316
01:04:50.912 --> 01:04:52.000
<v ROSAMORE>Mm hmm.

1317
01:04:52.000 --> 01:04:56.731
<v ARSENEAULT>What do you remember about like any home remedies your family was known for?

1318
01:04:56.731 --> 01:04:58.083
Like medicine and stuff?

1319
01:04:58.083 --> 01:05:03.153
<v ROSAMORE>Well, with my mom’s mom, whenever you would get cut, she would use spider webs.

1320
01:05:03.153 --> 01:05:07.546
You know, take a little piece of spider web off the corner wall,

1321
01:05:07.546 --> 01:05:12.278
and then she would put it on that cut and it will stop bleeding.

1322
01:05:12.278 --> 01:05:13.630
<v ARSENEAULT>I’ve heard of that.

1323
01:05:13.630 --> 01:05:15.319
<v ROSAMORE>And it would stop bleeding.

1324
01:05:15.319 --> 01:05:15.995
Oh, yeah.

1325
01:05:15.995 --> 01:05:23.431
Then I mainly remember because I remember when my brother was mad at me for something, I guess, because I was spoiled.

1326
01:05:23.431 --> 01:05:25.120
And I told my mom.

1327
01:05:25.120 --> 01:05:27.486
And I probably lied at the time.

1328
01:05:27.486 --> 01:05:31.542
Well, he got mad, and he was shoveling dirt for my mama.

1329
01:05:31.542 --> 01:05:36.949
And I kept running, I [makes teasing noise] and he cut my toe with the shovel.

1330
01:05:36.949 --> 01:05:41.343
Well, my grandmother come running out of the house with some spider web.

1331
01:05:41.343 --> 01:05:43.032
Stopped that cut, stopped bleeding.

1332
01:05:43.032 --> 01:05:46.412
And she just used this stuff they call monkey blood.

1333
01:05:46.412 --> 01:05:48.778
Some red stuff in a little bottle.

1334
01:05:48.778 --> 01:05:53.847
And she put it on there so it stopped it from hurting, stinging or whatever.

1335
01:05:53.847 --> 01:05:58.579
And it used to use sassafras and, what’s the name of that other root?

1336
01:05:58.579 --> 01:06:02.296
Used to use like a root, kind of like tree root.

1337
01:06:02.296 --> 01:06:05.000
But they would make tea with it.

1338
01:06:05.000 --> 01:06:05.947
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, the sassafras tea?

1339
01:06:05.947 --> 01:06:06.421
<v ROSAMORE>Uh huh.

1340
01:06:06.421 --> 01:06:06.895
<v ARSENEAULT>Mamou tea?

1341
01:06:06.895 --> 01:06:07.368
<v ROSAMORE>Mamou tea.

1342
01:06:07.368 --> 01:06:07.605
Yeah.

1343
01:06:07.605 --> 01:06:10.684
But it had like a little root that they would put in water.

1344
01:06:10.684 --> 01:06:12.105
<v ARSENEAULT>Is that sassafras root or something?

1345
01:06:12.105 --> 01:06:12.579
<v ROSAMORE>Uh huh.

1346
01:06:12.579 --> 01:06:14.000
You drop it in water.

1347
01:06:14.000 --> 01:06:16.561
<v ARSENEAULT>And you grew it in your yard?

1348
01:06:16.561 --> 01:06:17.293
<v ROSAMORE>Uh huh.

1349
01:06:17.293 --> 01:06:22.415
Or you can go in the woods and you can find it. [. . . ?] back then,

1350
01:06:22.415 --> 01:06:29.366
because they pretty much know, they pretty much know what was mamou tea root, what was sassafras tea root.

1351
01:06:29.366 --> 01:06:32.293
And they would just go and pick them.

1352
01:06:32.293 --> 01:06:35.585
And then they would put them in the pot.

1353
01:06:35.585 --> 01:06:36.683
Let it boil.

1354
01:06:36.683 --> 01:06:38.878
And then you drink the tea.

1355
01:06:38.878 --> 01:06:41.439
You put honey in it, you know.

1356
01:06:41.439 --> 01:06:46.195
And those that really wanted to add [unclear] put alcohol in it, too.

1357
01:06:46.195 --> 01:06:48.756
Put like cloves and stuff like that.

1358
01:06:48.756 --> 01:06:50.951
<v ARSENEAULT>Like a hot toddy or something?

1359
01:06:50.951 --> 01:06:52.049
<v ROSAMORE>Yeah. [. . .?] so much.

1360
01:06:52.049 --> 01:06:54.244
<v ARSENEAULT>What if you had a fever?

1361
01:06:54.244 --> 01:07:02.293
<v ROSAMORE>Oh, shoot, they would give us, they would give us, if we had a cold or flu, they would give us soup.

1362
01:07:02.293 --> 01:07:11.439
Throwing up, they would give us, throwing up or diarrhea, they would give us crackers, Saltine crackers, with Sprite, or Saltine crackers with Coke, Coca-Cola.

1363
01:07:11.439 --> 01:07:16.561
And we would sit, what they said, or this how they would do it.

1364
01:07:16.561 --> 01:07:19.122
They would, how do that saying go?

1365
01:07:19.122 --> 01:07:21.317
Starve a cold, feed a fever?

1366
01:07:21.317 --> 01:07:22.415
Kind of like?

1367
01:07:22.415 --> 01:07:25.707
Well, back then, you know, they still had stuff,

1368
01:07:25.707 --> 01:07:26.805
<c.p>medicines for fever.

1369
01:07:26.805 --> 01:07:32.293
But they would give you, they would keep you home from school and they would,

1370
01:07:32.293 --> 01:07:36.683
you know, dope you up with a lot of soup and liquids.

1371
01:07:36.683 --> 01:07:38.512
And you would get better.

1372
01:07:38.512 --> 01:07:41.073
But now they got all that antibiotics.

1373
01:07:41.073 --> 01:07:44.000
And back then, we had no antibiotics.

1374
01:07:44.000 --> 01:07:46.584
<v ARSENEAULT>What if someone got really, really sick?

1375
01:07:46.584 --> 01:07:47.322
What happened?

1376
01:07:47.322 --> 01:07:49.906
<v ROSAMORE>They would take them to a hospital.

1377
01:07:49.906 --> 01:07:51.013
<v ARSENEAULT>In Lake Charles?

1378
01:07:51.013 --> 01:07:52.121
<v ROSAMORE>Mm hmm, Sulphur.

1379
01:07:52.121 --> 01:07:53.228
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, in Sulphur.

1380
01:07:53.228 --> 01:07:55.074
<v ROSAMORE>Yeah, because it was closer.

1381
01:07:55.074 --> 01:07:59.872
And if it was a real, real, now see, back in the day,

1382
01:07:59.872 --> 01:08:05.040
I don't remember this, but my mom’s mother and sister used to deliver children.

1383
01:08:05.040 --> 01:08:10.208
You know, instead of going to a hospital, they had what they call midwives.

1384
01:08:10.208 --> 01:08:13.530
And they would actually deliver your baby at home.

1385
01:08:13.530 --> 01:08:19.805
Well, because I think my mama say that her aunt delivered her and her twin at home.

1386
01:08:19.805 --> 01:08:27.926
And now when I was old enough to remember, I remembered about no midwife coming [know how?] to deliver the baby now.

1387
01:08:27.926 --> 01:08:30.141
<v ARSENEAULT>Were you delivered in the home?

1388
01:08:30.141 --> 01:08:30.510
<v ROSAMORE>Huh?

1389
01:08:30.510 --> 01:08:32.725
<v ARSENEAULT>Were you delivered in the house?

1390
01:08:32.725 --> 01:08:34.940
<v ROSAMORE>They usually deliver in houses, yeah.

1391
01:08:34.940 --> 01:08:36.047
<v ARSENEAULT>But you were?

1392
01:08:36.047 --> 01:08:39.000
<v ROSAMORE>No. I was delivered at Moss Regional.

1393
01:08:39.000 --> 01:08:42.290
<v ARSENEAULT>So like your mom’s generation got delivered in the house.

1394
01:08:42.290 --> 01:08:42.619
<v ROSAMORE>Yes.

1395
01:08:42.619 --> 01:08:42.948
Yes.

1396
01:08:42.948 --> 01:08:43.277
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1397
01:08:43.277 --> 01:08:44.265
<v ROSAMORE>And my dad.

1398
01:08:44.265 --> 01:08:48.542
You know, all of them was actually born in the house by midwives.

1399
01:08:48.542 --> 01:08:51.503
And there was always a midwife in the family.

1400
01:08:51.503 --> 01:08:52.161
<v ARSENEAULT>That’s convenient.

1401
01:08:52.161 --> 01:08:52.490
<v ROSAMORE>Huh?

1402
01:08:52.490 --> 01:08:53.148
<v ARSENEAULT>That’s convenient.

1403
01:08:53.148 --> 01:08:53.477
<v ROSAMORE>Yeah.

1404
01:08:53.477 --> 01:09:02.032
It was always a sister or somebody that was taught by your grandma, you know, that taught you how to bring a baby in the house.

1405
01:09:02.032 --> 01:09:07.297
How to actually boil the water, you know, like you see on TV how they do.

1406
01:09:07.297 --> 01:09:10.258
Well, that’s what they was taught by their mother.

1407
01:09:10.258 --> 01:09:12.232
And they just carry it down.

1408
01:09:12.232 --> 01:09:14.865
And on my daddy’s side, the same way.

1409
01:09:14.865 --> 01:09:16.839
Because my grandma had fifteen children.

1410
01:09:16.839 --> 01:09:18.813
My dad’s mama had fifteen children.

1411
01:09:18.813 --> 01:09:21.774
So you know it was a lot of delivering.

1412
01:09:21.774 --> 01:09:24.077
So somebody had to come to us.

1413
01:09:24.077 --> 01:09:30.000
They had no, by the time we would have got to the hospital in horse and buggy—

1414
01:09:30.000 --> 01:09:31.971
<v ARSENEAULT>The baby would have come.

1415
01:09:31.971 --> 01:09:35.912
<v ROSAMORE>And then the doctors used to come home visit, too.

1416
01:09:35.912 --> 01:09:39.065
They used to come and do home visits.

1417
01:09:39.065 --> 01:09:42.612
So, and then doctor [unclear] and delivered the baby.

1418
01:09:42.612 --> 01:09:45.371
You know, with the boil the water.

1419
01:09:45.371 --> 01:09:51.282
Kind of like, you ever watch that little series called Little House on the Prairie?

1420
01:09:51.282 --> 01:09:54.829
Okay, well that’s pretty much back in that time.

1421
01:09:54.829 --> 01:10:01.529
You know, where they would actually, the doctor would do house visits, and come bring you medicine.

1422
01:10:01.529 --> 01:10:05.865
And you just give them a little something for the exchange.

1423
01:10:05.865 --> 01:10:11.776
Or just actually, the little baby, put the water on, let’s get the towels ready.

1424
01:10:11.776 --> 01:10:14.141
That’s how it was back then.

1425
01:10:14.141 --> 01:10:16.506
Not when I was born, though.

1426
01:10:16.506 --> 01:10:20.447
Because I born at the charity hospital in Lake Charles.

1427
01:10:20.447 --> 01:10:22.812
But my brother born in Sulphur.

1428
01:10:22.812 --> 01:10:25.176
Yeah but oh, I tell you.

1429
01:10:25.176 --> 01:10:26.753
<v ARSENEAULT>It’s a different time.

1430
01:10:26.753 --> 01:10:28.329
<v ROSAMORE>Oh, yeah, majorly different.

1431
01:10:28.329 --> 01:10:29.118
You know?

1432
01:10:29.118 --> 01:10:29.906
Majorly different.

1433
01:10:29.906 --> 01:10:32.665
<v ARSENEAULT>What did you do for like mosquitoes?

1434
01:10:32.665 --> 01:10:35.029
Were there a lot of mosquitoes?

1435
01:10:35.029 --> 01:10:37.000
Mosquito bites and things?

1436
01:10:37.000 --> 01:10:38.657
<v ROSAMORE>Oh, well, first of all,

1437
01:10:38.657 --> 01:10:48.269
you would try to put OFF! [bug repellant] on so you don’t get bit by no damn mosquitoes. [laughs] But it wasn’t that lucky all the time, you know.

1438
01:10:48.269 --> 01:10:53.240
My dad used to, oh, he used to rub us down with alcohol, you know?

1439
01:10:53.240 --> 01:10:57.880
And if you was allergic to mosquitoes, well, but none of us was allergic.

1440
01:10:57.880 --> 01:10:59.869
It was mostly wasps and bees.

1441
01:10:59.869 --> 01:11:03.514
A lot of people was allergic to it, like my cousin.

1442
01:11:03.514 --> 01:11:08.817
The one come to the door with his eyes like this. [laughter] I’ll never forget that.

1443
01:11:08.817 --> 01:11:12.131
But mosquitoes weren’t as plentiful as they is now, though.

1444
01:11:12.131 --> 01:11:15.777
Because I really don’t remember that much about the mosquitoes, really.

1445
01:11:15.777 --> 01:11:19.091
Not unless we was going fishing early in the morning.

1446
01:11:19.091 --> 01:11:22.737
We would spray ourselves down with OFF! and they wouldn’t bother.

1447
01:11:22.737 --> 01:11:26.051
But other than that, now you can’t even go outside,

1448
01:11:26.051 --> 01:11:31.686
you’ve got to buy a damn mosquito plant to do this with to spray that stuff out.

1449
01:11:31.686 --> 01:11:32.349
<v ARSENEAULT>Mm hmm.

1450
01:11:32.349 --> 01:11:34.669
Or like that machine that zaps them.

1451
01:11:34.669 --> 01:11:35.000
<v ROSAMORE>Yeah.

1452
01:11:35.000 --> 01:11:35.331
Yeah.

1453
01:11:35.331 --> 01:11:37.320
Really. [laughs] Mine don’t work, though.

1454
01:11:37.320 --> 01:11:37.983
For real.

1455
01:11:37.983 --> 01:11:39.971
<v ARSENEAULT>What do you remember about school?

1456
01:11:39.971 --> 01:11:42.291
You said you were in the band.

1457
01:11:42.291 --> 01:11:43.949
<v ROSAMORE>I was in the band.

1458
01:11:43.949 --> 01:11:45.937
I used to sing in chorus.

1459
01:11:45.937 --> 01:11:48.257
I used to, let me see, okay.

1460
01:11:48.257 --> 01:11:50.577
I used to play in the band.

1461
01:11:50.577 --> 01:11:55.880
I played every instrument except for the [bone tone?] and the baritone, I don’t like those.

1462
01:11:55.880 --> 01:11:58.863
But I played the flute, I played the clarinet.

1463
01:11:58.863 --> 01:12:01.846
I even played the, what was that other one?

1464
01:12:01.846 --> 01:12:07.811
I even tried to play, I know for sure I played the clarinet and I played the flute.

1465
01:12:07.811 --> 01:12:09.469
And I loved that clarinet.

1466
01:12:09.469 --> 01:12:11.457
It just had that beautiful sound.

1467
01:12:11.457 --> 01:12:16.429
Anyway, we used to go to different little schools and do our concerts, you know?

1468
01:12:16.429 --> 01:12:18.417
And I used to like that.

1469
01:12:18.417 --> 01:12:23.057
We used to have what they call, I don't know if you call it,

1470
01:12:23.057 --> 01:12:29.023
not a record hop at the school, but it was like a, oh, god darn, what was that?

1471
01:12:29.023 --> 01:12:33.000
Where we used to all practice for this big old event.

1472
01:12:33.000 --> 01:12:34.476
<v ARSENEAULT>Like parish choir or [. . . ?].

1473
01:12:34.476 --> 01:12:35.214
<v ROSAMORE>At school.

1474
01:12:35.214 --> 01:12:40.381
You know, and then we would all be doing different little things, you know.

1475
01:12:40.381 --> 01:12:43.333
Dancing or singing or, kind of like a—

1476
01:12:43.333 --> 01:12:44.071
<v ARSENEAULT>Talent show?

1477
01:12:44.071 --> 01:12:45.179
<v ROSAMORE>Yeah, talent show.

1478
01:12:45.179 --> 01:12:47.024
<v ARSENEAULT>Kind of like that, yeah.

1479
01:12:47.024 --> 01:12:48.869
<v ROSAMORE>That’s what it was, yeah.

1480
01:12:48.869 --> 01:12:49.976
A talent show.

1481
01:12:49.976 --> 01:12:57.726
And I remember when the nurseries used to come to school and give us shots for polio and all that crap.

1482
01:12:57.726 --> 01:12:58.833
In the cafeteria.

1483
01:12:58.833 --> 01:13:03.631
And I’m like, really? [laughs] But anyway, they used to give us shots.

1484
01:13:03.631 --> 01:13:06.214
Oh, yeah, I used to do a

1485
01:13:06.214 --> 01:13:08.060
<c.p>lot of things at school.

1486
01:13:08.060 --> 01:13:15.810
Then they used to have the, what did they call it, the beauty pageant, where they would pick out the queens.

1487
01:13:15.810 --> 01:13:20.238
And I had been the queen for Mossville when I was younger.

1488
01:13:20.238 --> 01:13:26.512
You had to raise money, so my mama did wienie roasts and sold popcorn balls you know,

1489
01:13:26.512 --> 01:13:32.048
just to build up my money so I could get my dress and all that.

1490
01:13:32.048 --> 01:13:33.893
And I remember them days.

1491
01:13:33.893 --> 01:13:42.012
I used to play in the band, I used to sew, I used to be in home ec., I used to sew.

1492
01:13:42.012 --> 01:13:46.810
Can’t sew good, though, but I did get in the home economics class.

1493
01:13:46.810 --> 01:13:49.393
But I really liked the band, though.

1494
01:13:49.393 --> 01:13:50.869
I really liked that.

1495
01:13:50.869 --> 01:13:52.345
Because I like music.

1496
01:13:52.345 --> 01:13:55.298
And I used to be in the choir.

1497
01:13:55.298 --> 01:13:58.619
I used to be in the choir, you know?

1498
01:13:58.619 --> 01:14:06.000
And I didn’t really care too much for the choir, because I didn’t like that kind of singing. [laughs]

1499
01:14:06.000 --> 01:14:07.882
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, like the proper singing?

1500
01:14:07.882 --> 01:14:09.765
<v ROSAMORE>No. I want to sing.

1501
01:14:09.765 --> 01:14:16.165
I mean, you know, pretty much like some of us grew up already know how to dance.

1502
01:14:16.165 --> 01:14:18.800
They had to practice, you know, singing.

1503
01:14:18.800 --> 01:14:20.682
We were born to sing.

1504
01:14:20.682 --> 01:14:24.824
We would sing at home more than we would anywhere else.

1505
01:14:24.824 --> 01:14:25.576
And church.

1506
01:14:25.576 --> 01:14:28.965
But this other woman was kind of like, hmmm,

1507
01:14:28.965 --> 01:14:36.118
but I had to have some type of subject to get past to another point, so I took chorus.

1508
01:14:36.118 --> 01:14:37.624
And like geez, Louise.

1509
01:14:37.624 --> 01:14:39.882
But it was okay, I guess.

1510
01:14:39.882 --> 01:14:41.765
<v ARSENEAULT>I guess you made it.

1511
01:14:41.765 --> 01:14:42.141
<v ROSAMORE>Yeah.

1512
01:14:42.141 --> 01:14:43.647
I went through it.

1513
01:14:43.647 --> 01:14:45.906
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember any particular teachers?

1514
01:14:45.906 --> 01:14:46.282
<v ROSAMORE>Yeah.

1515
01:14:46.282 --> 01:14:47.788
I remember Mrs. [Shacherie?].

1516
01:14:47.788 --> 01:14:50.800
I think her name now is Ms. Perry.

1517
01:14:50.800 --> 01:14:51.929
She’s still living.

1518
01:14:51.929 --> 01:14:54.188
I see her all the time.

1519
01:14:54.188 --> 01:14:56.824
She was a real, real nice teacher.

1520
01:14:56.824 --> 01:14:59.459
Then I had my first grade teacher.

1521
01:14:59.459 --> 01:15:01.718
I might have said that before.

1522
01:15:01.718 --> 01:15:02.471
Ms. Beverly.

1523
01:15:02.471 --> 01:15:03.976
<v ARSENEAULT>I think you did.

1524
01:15:03.976 --> 01:15:04.729
Mm hmm.

1525
01:15:04.729 --> 01:15:07.365
<v ROSAMORE>Died when I was in first grade.

1526
01:15:07.365 --> 01:15:10.000
<v ARSENEAULT>And you remember that ever since.

1527
01:15:10.000 --> 01:15:10.667
<v ROSAMORE>Oh, yeah.

1528
01:15:10.667 --> 01:15:12.333
And I never forgot that.

1529
01:15:12.333 --> 01:15:16.000
And then I had Mister, um, Ms. Franklin was our library.

1530
01:15:16.000 --> 01:15:17.333
She was really nice.

1531
01:15:17.333 --> 01:15:19.333
Ms. Reynolds was our science teacher.

1532
01:15:19.333 --> 01:15:21.000
She was real, real nice.

1533
01:15:21.000 --> 01:15:23.333
And I think she’s still living, too.

1534
01:15:23.333 --> 01:15:25.667
Because somebody said they had seen her.

1535
01:15:25.667 --> 01:15:28.000
<v ARSENEAULT>I think you did talk about this.

1536
01:15:28.000 --> 01:15:29.333
<v ROSAMORE>I think I did.

1537
01:15:29.333 --> 01:15:32.000
<v ARSENEAULT>On that, I think you said that already.

1538
01:15:32.000 --> 01:15:33.333
<v ROSAMORE>I think I did.

1539
01:15:33.333 --> 01:15:36.333
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember any like healers in the area?

1540
01:15:36.333 --> 01:15:38.000
Were there any like healing?

1541
01:15:38.000 --> 01:15:39.333
<v ROSAMORE>What you mean healers?

1542
01:15:39.333 --> 01:15:39.667
Like—

1543
01:15:39.667 --> 01:15:43.333
<v ARSENEAULT>Like not a doctor, but somebody like a, like a healer.

1544
01:15:43.333 --> 01:15:46.667
<v ROSAMORE>Well my daddy’s brother was one of those. [. . . ?] still lives.

1545
01:15:46.667 --> 01:15:49.667
My uncle [. . . ?] That’s what I was told growing up.

1546
01:15:49.667 --> 01:15:53.667
You know, they have a little child, had a rash or whatever,

1547
01:15:53.667 --> 01:16:01.000
just bring them to my Uncle [Muti?] and he’ll blow in their mouth three times and then the rash will go away.

1548
01:16:01.000 --> 01:16:02.667
And they said that work.

1549
01:16:02.667 --> 01:16:08.333
In fact, I brought my daughter whenever, my oldest daughter whenever she got sick with a rash.

1550
01:16:08.333 --> 01:16:10.667
And that’s what my dad has said.

1551
01:16:10.667 --> 01:16:13.333
“Well, you need to bring her to Muti.”

1552
01:16:13.333 --> 01:16:19.333
So I brought them to my Uncle Muti and my Uncle Muti blow in their mouth three times.

1553
01:16:19.333 --> 01:16:23.000
And next thing I knew, their rash was gone, baby.

1554
01:16:23.000 --> 01:16:23.366
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

1555
01:16:23.366 --> 01:16:24.832
<v ROSAMORE>Their rash had looked [. . . ?].

1556
01:16:24.832 --> 01:16:31.062
But they say somebody like that, and they never, they was born and never seen their father,

1557
01:16:31.062 --> 01:16:34.727
those are the ones that are blessed with that healing.

1558
01:16:34.727 --> 01:16:39.491
And he never did, which he never, ever got to know his dad.

1559
01:16:39.491 --> 01:16:43.522
Because my grandma was pregnant for him whenever my grandpa died.

1560
01:16:43.522 --> 01:16:50.851
Of a, I want to say he lost his mind or something and then he end up going to jail.

1561
01:16:50.851 --> 01:16:56.714
And then they kept him for seven days and then he didn’t eat for seven days.

1562
01:16:56.714 --> 01:17:02.944
And when he got home, my grandma had no, my dad mom, she didn’t know, he kept

1563
01:17:02.944 --> 01:17:06.975
<c.p>[unclear] but he had really lost his mind at that point.

1564
01:17:06.975 --> 01:17:11.006
But she went and fixed him a big plate of food.

1565
01:17:11.006 --> 01:17:15.037
And when he got halfway through it, he smothered to death.

1566
01:17:15.037 --> 01:17:15.404
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

1567
01:17:15.404 --> 01:17:17.236
Because he ate too much.

1568
01:17:17.236 --> 01:17:18.702
<v ROSAMORE>He ate too fast.

1569
01:17:18.702 --> 01:17:20.168
<v ARSENEAULT>He ate too fast.

1570
01:17:20.168 --> 01:17:22.000
On an empty stomach.

1571
01:17:22.000 --> 01:17:22.676
<v ROSAMORE>Mm hmm.

1572
01:17:22.676 --> 01:17:24.703
And he had been seven days.

1573
01:17:24.703 --> 01:17:29.770
And do you know, after that, my dad used to tell us all the time,

1574
01:17:29.770 --> 01:17:36.527
“I don’t mind you not eating two days, but you better not try to eat no more than two days.”

1575
01:17:36.527 --> 01:17:39.230
But he said, “Seven days, that’s not good.

1576
01:17:39.230 --> 01:17:40.919
You ain’t going to last.

1577
01:17:40.919 --> 01:17:42.946
You ain’t going to make it.”

1578
01:17:42.946 --> 01:17:47.676
But we know, now that I’m older, I know now why he said that.

1579
01:17:47.676 --> 01:17:49.027
<v ARSENEAULT>Because of his dad.

1580
01:17:49.027 --> 01:17:51.054
<v ROSAMORE>Because his dad died from it.

1581
01:17:51.054 --> 01:17:51.392
Yeah.

1582
01:17:51.392 --> 01:17:56.122
And see, because I know for sure it was my uncle was a healer.

1583
01:17:56.122 --> 01:18:02.203
But I know there was a man that was from [Westlake?] that was a healer, too, they say.

1584
01:18:02.203 --> 01:18:04.568
But I never got to know him.

1585
01:18:04.568 --> 01:18:06.257
But I know my uncle.

1586
01:18:06.257 --> 01:18:08.284
They always say my uncle was.

1587
01:18:08.284 --> 01:18:10.986
<v ARSENEAULT>He’s just been a healer his whole life?

1588
01:18:10.986 --> 01:18:11.662
<v ROSAMORE>Mm hmm.

1589
01:18:11.662 --> 01:18:12.000
Yep.

1590
01:18:12.000 --> 01:18:12.676
Pretty much.

1591
01:18:12.676 --> 01:18:17.405
But you know now, I don't think he even think about it, you know?

1592
01:18:17.405 --> 01:18:19.770
Because he’s getting up in age now.

1593
01:18:19.770 --> 01:18:21.122
He’s in his mid-sixties.

1594
01:18:21.122 --> 01:18:21.459
So.

1595
01:18:21.459 --> 01:18:23.486
<v ARSENEAULT>Does he still live in Mossville?

1596
01:18:23.486 --> 01:18:24.162
<v ROSAMORE>Mm hmm.

1597
01:18:24.162 --> 01:18:27.203
He’s one of them that don’t want to leave.

1598
01:18:27.203 --> 01:18:29.230
Like really, but he won’t leave.

1599
01:18:29.230 --> 01:18:34.635
He said no. But he going to eventually leave, because he won’t be able to stay.

1600
01:18:34.635 --> 01:18:36.324
I mean, really, it’s really.

1601
01:18:36.324 --> 01:18:44.432
And I don’t fault Sasol because, as a matter of fact, I really feel like there was a blessing to a lot of us.

1602
01:18:44.432 --> 01:18:49.500
Many of my old relatives, you know, they’ve had homes ever since I was little.

1603
01:18:49.500 --> 01:18:55.919
And homes going down but they can’t afford to fix them because they [all for?] one income, you know?

1604
01:18:55.919 --> 01:18:58.959
I think there’s a blessing. [. . . ?] better than that, really.

1605
01:18:58.959 --> 01:19:02.000
But some people is very upset about it.

1606
01:19:02.000 --> 01:19:06.082
<v ARSENEAULT>Maybe they don’t want to, they’re scared to leave or something.

1607
01:19:06.082 --> 01:19:06.454
Yeah.

1608
01:19:06.454 --> 01:19:06.825
<v ROSAMORE>Yeah.

1609
01:19:06.825 --> 01:19:13.876
And I think it’s, because like I said, because we was, you can always take your memories with you.

1610
01:19:13.876 --> 01:19:17.216
And you can make new memories where you go.

1611
01:19:17.216 --> 01:19:20.557
And I don't think that’s where the problem is.

1612
01:19:20.557 --> 01:19:23.155
It’s just, I don't know, but anyway.

1613
01:19:23.155 --> 01:19:25.381
It’s, it was interesting, living there.

1614
01:19:25.381 --> 01:19:26.124
You know?

1615
01:19:26.124 --> 01:19:32.804
And you know, I didn’t realize that I was kin to almost everybody until I got real older.

1616
01:19:32.804 --> 01:19:38.000
I said, lord, good thing I wasn’t trying to date nobody [. . . ?], you know.

1617
01:19:38.000 --> 01:19:40.595
<v ARSENEAULT>[laughs] You might marry a cousin or something.

1618
01:19:40.595 --> 01:19:42.865
<v ROSAMORE>Oh, girl! [. . . ?] I would be like, what?

1619
01:19:42.865 --> 01:19:45.784
They’d be like, we didn’t act cool, you know?

1620
01:19:45.784 --> 01:19:47.405
But if you don’t know.

1621
01:19:47.405 --> 01:19:50.973
Back then, they didn’t tell you a lot about your relatives.

1622
01:19:50.973 --> 01:19:52.270
<v ARSENEAULT>I guess it happens.

1623
01:19:52.270 --> 01:19:57.784
<v ROSAMORE>The only ones I knew I was related to was the ones that was living in Mossville.

1624
01:19:57.784 --> 01:20:02.000
But there were some I was related to that was outside of Mossville.

1625
01:20:02.000 --> 01:20:03.622
You didn’t have no idea.

1626
01:20:03.622 --> 01:20:05.892
And learn as you go, I guess.

1627
01:20:05.892 --> 01:20:06.216
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

1628
01:20:06.216 --> 01:20:07.838
Like it’s one big family.

1629
01:20:07.838 --> 01:20:08.162
<v ROSAMORE>Yeah.

1630
01:20:08.162 --> 01:20:08.486
Yeah.

1631
01:20:08.486 --> 01:20:10.108
Every Braxton you came to.

1632
01:20:10.108 --> 01:20:11.730
Every Rigmaiden you came to.

1633
01:20:11.730 --> 01:20:13.351
Every Mosses you came to.

1634
01:20:13.351 --> 01:20:14.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

1635
01:20:14.000 --> 01:20:14.726
<v ROSAMORE>Every Perkins.

1636
01:20:14.726 --> 01:20:20.170
This is what we were ever told, if he a Braxton, he kin to you.

1637
01:20:20.170 --> 01:20:23.074
If he a Perkins, he kin to you.

1638
01:20:23.074 --> 01:20:25.978
I said, okay, whatever, I’m related to everybody.

1639
01:20:25.978 --> 01:20:30.333
And back then, because we ain’t had no horse and buggies, us,

1640
01:20:30.333 --> 01:20:36.504
because I remember when my daddy said that his brother got killed in DeQuincy, his oldest brother.

1641
01:20:36.504 --> 01:20:41.585
He said they had to go by horse and buggy, his mama and them.

1642
01:20:41.585 --> 01:20:47.756
And said looked like it took them days, but it really was just a bunch of hours.

1643
01:20:47.756 --> 01:20:51.385
But all the way from Mossville to DeQuincy on horseback.

1644
01:20:51.385 --> 01:20:52.111
And not

1645
01:20:52.111 --> 01:20:55.015
<c.p>horseback, but on the buggy in the wagon.

1646
01:20:55.015 --> 01:20:56.467
That’s a slow ride.

1647
01:20:56.467 --> 01:21:00.822
And had to go through a trail road to get to DeQuincy.

1648
01:21:00.822 --> 01:21:01.911
Times have changed.

1649
01:21:01.911 --> 01:21:03.000
So much.

1650
01:21:03.000 --> 01:21:09.400
<v ARSENEAULT>What’s the one big thing you want people to remember about Mossville?

1651
01:21:09.400 --> 01:21:21.667
<v ROSAMORE>The one big thing to me would be, is that it was, to me it was the best place to raise my children.

1652
01:21:21.667 --> 01:21:27.533
Didn’t have to worry about your children are straying and becoming,

1653
01:21:27.533 --> 01:21:43.533
just getting out there in the world because you had so much you could do with them to keep them from wanting to go out there and get into trouble.

1654
01:21:43.533 --> 01:21:51.533
And me personally, I grew up there and I know it was a loving place.

1655
01:21:51.533 --> 01:22:03.267
If I had a choice, I would have never [coughs] excuse me, I would have never moved, because I was happy there.

1656
01:22:03.267 --> 01:22:05.400
I felt love there.

1657
01:22:05.400 --> 01:22:07.000
Everybody showed love.

1658
01:22:07.000 --> 01:22:11.267
You know, all your family members showed love.

1659
01:22:11.267 --> 01:22:16.067
And you can’t find that in too many places.

1660
01:22:16.067 --> 01:22:18.733
No matter where you go.

1661
01:22:18.733 --> 01:22:26.200
You can’t find somebody on a truly love you for you and all that.

1662
01:22:26.200 --> 01:22:32.067
Hold on just a second. [coughs] Must have got dry there.

1663
01:22:32.067 --> 01:22:33.133
Mm hmm.

1664
01:22:33.133 --> 01:22:34.200
<v ARSENEAULT>You good?

1665
01:22:34.200 --> 01:22:39.000
You’ve been talking for an hour and twenty-two minutes.

1666
01:22:39.000 --> 01:22:42.200
So that might be why. [laughs]

1667
01:22:42.200 --> 01:22:47.000
<v ROSAMORE>Mm hmm. [coughs] It’s that last, a little choke.

1668
01:22:47.000 --> 01:22:53.400
Okay, now you ask me what would I want everybody to know?

1669
01:22:53.400 --> 01:22:55.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Mm hmm.

1670
01:22:55.000 --> 01:23:04.231
<v ROSAMORE>I would say that, you know, if you want to raise your kids, if you wanted to raise your kids in a good environment,

1671
01:23:04.231 --> 01:23:10.000
not worry about your kids getting into trouble, Mossville would have been the best place.

1672
01:23:10.000 --> 01:23:11.923
It was in the country.

1673
01:23:11.923 --> 01:23:13.077
It was quiet.

1674
01:23:13.077 --> 01:23:18.846
It was away from a lot of things that could make problems for you here.

1675
01:23:18.846 --> 01:23:23.077
It was a place, it was a town of its own.

1676
01:23:23.077 --> 01:23:28.077
But everybody loved each other, watched everybody, watched everybody’s kids. [coughs] Excuse me.

1677
01:23:28.077 --> 01:23:34.615
And tried to do things, family things, to keep the kids’ mind off of getting in trouble.

1678
01:23:34.615 --> 01:23:36.154
All kind of stuff.

1679
01:23:36.154 --> 01:23:44.615
And then the kids always had things they could do, like go fishing, go hunting, and the girl, well the boys, especially.

1680
01:23:44.615 --> 01:23:48.462
And the girls would help the moms around the house.

1681
01:23:48.462 --> 01:23:49.615
Cook the meals.

1682
01:23:49.615 --> 01:23:57.692
And so it’s kind of like we just grew up knowing how to make it if ever we would have to.

1683
01:23:57.692 --> 01:23:59.231
Later on, you know.

1684
01:23:59.231 --> 01:23:59.615
Learn.

1685
01:23:59.615 --> 01:24:01.923
We would learn how to survive.

1686
01:24:01.923 --> 01:24:05.385
And I’m sure there’s more towns that does that.

1687
01:24:05.385 --> 01:24:13.846
But a small town is very important when everybody, you pull together and make us a bad thing, be a good thing.

1688
01:24:13.846 --> 01:24:20.385
All you do is watch over everybody’s kids and you just make sure that— [coughs] Good lord.

1689
01:24:20.385 --> 01:24:25.769
You know, and just make sure that everything and everybody is taken care of.

1690
01:24:25.769 --> 01:24:33.077
And to me, if I had the choice, I would have never moved, because that’s where I grew up.

1691
01:24:33.077 --> 01:24:36.154
And I grew up with all that love.

1692
01:24:36.154 --> 01:24:38.846
When you move, you don’t find that.

1693
01:24:38.846 --> 01:24:43.077
Because you find different, what you find are people act different.

1694
01:24:43.077 --> 01:24:48.077
You’re not from their town, so don’t feel like you’re part of them.

1695
01:24:48.077 --> 01:24:50.000
You know what I’m saying?

1696
01:24:50.000 --> 01:24:50.385
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

1697
01:24:50.385 --> 01:24:57.308
<v ROSAMORE>So to go from where I came from to go to here is like, it’s a major difference.

1698
01:24:57.308 --> 01:24:58.462
A major difference.

1699
01:24:58.462 --> 01:25:01.538
But I’ll always have Mossville in my memory.

1700
01:25:01.538 --> 01:25:02.692
And I always,

1701
01:25:02.692 --> 01:25:10.769
<c.p>I’ve always took pictures, I don't know why I did them then, but now I’m glad I did because you know,

1702
01:25:10.769 --> 01:25:15.769
I’ve got pictures of different areas of Mossville with my kids and me,

1703
01:25:15.769 --> 01:25:22.308
or just me sometimes, you know, that I can always go back and look at, you know?

1704
01:25:22.308 --> 01:25:25.000
And I can always show my grandkids.

1705
01:25:25.000 --> 01:25:32.308
And they were never, if they happen to me, then my grandkids would carry on that to their children.

1706
01:25:32.308 --> 01:25:39.231
If they don’t know where I really lived, but they’ll have pictures to kind of get an idea.

1707
01:25:39.231 --> 01:25:45.000
Where the rec was, the pool, this old store was a long time ago.

1708
01:25:45.000 --> 01:25:46.797
<v ARSENEAULT>And they’ll have this, too.

1709
01:25:46.797 --> 01:25:47.876
All your stories.

1710
01:25:47.876 --> 01:25:48.235
<v ROSAMORE>Huh?

1711
01:25:48.235 --> 01:25:49.673
<v ARSENEAULT>They’ll have this, too.

1712
01:25:49.673 --> 01:25:50.752
The oral history.

1713
01:25:50.752 --> 01:25:52.190
<v ROSAMORE>Well, that’s true, too.

1714
01:25:52.190 --> 01:25:59.379
So you know, now, what I thank God, and I thank Sasol for actually letting this happen, because you know,

1715
01:25:59.379 --> 01:26:08.725
because I think there’s a reason why a lot of us did not want to move, because we did not want our town to be forgotten.

1716
01:26:08.725 --> 01:26:14.118
Like you live there, but now we’ve got to take your property, so it’s gone.

1717
01:26:14.118 --> 01:26:18.072
And that’s why a lot of people didn't want to go.

1718
01:26:18.072 --> 01:26:22.026
Because we felt like my God, I’ve been there fifty-three years.

1719
01:26:22.026 --> 01:26:29.216
My children will never know where, my children will, but their children will never know where I really come from.

1720
01:26:29.216 --> 01:26:31.732
But just a little town called Mossville.

1721
01:26:31.732 --> 01:26:35.327
And I heard they’re going to turn it into Mapleville.

1722
01:26:35.327 --> 01:26:36.765
<v ARSENEAULT>I didn't hear that.

1723
01:26:36.765 --> 01:26:37.124
<v ROSAMORE>Yeah.

1724
01:26:37.124 --> 01:26:39.281
It was somewhere in the paper.

1725
01:26:39.281 --> 01:26:40.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Mapleville.

1726
01:26:40.000 --> 01:26:40.353
<v ROSAMORE>Mapleville.

1727
01:26:40.353 --> 01:26:41.765
You know like Maplewood?

1728
01:26:41.765 --> 01:26:42.471
<v ARSENEAULT>Mm hmm.

1729
01:26:42.471 --> 01:26:44.588
<v ROSAMORE>Because Maplewood is right next over.

1730
01:26:44.588 --> 01:26:47.412
<v ARSENEAULT>So it’s the two names combined, Maple and—

1731
01:26:47.412 --> 01:26:49.882
<v ROSAMORE>So now it’s going to be Maplewood.

1732
01:26:49.882 --> 01:26:53.412
No, Mapleville, because Maplewood is already out here in Sulphur.

1733
01:26:53.412 --> 01:26:56.588
And I’m like, why would they change the name?

1734
01:26:56.588 --> 01:27:01.176
I think they building new homes and all that for all these workers.

1735
01:27:01.176 --> 01:27:04.353
And I think that’s why they change the name.

1736
01:27:04.353 --> 01:27:04.706
Mapleville.

1737
01:27:04.706 --> 01:27:05.765
<v ARSENEAULT>I got you.

1738
01:27:05.765 --> 01:27:09.294
<v ROSAMORE>But it’s going to always be Mossville to some, so.

1739
01:27:09.294 --> 01:27:10.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

1740
01:27:10.000 --> 01:27:13.313
<v ROSAMORE>You know, they can change the name, they can do whatever.

1741
01:27:13.313 --> 01:27:17.530
But to those like me and those that’s older, it will always be Mossville.

1742
01:27:17.530 --> 01:27:22.048
We might not look like it ever again, but in our hearts it will be.

1743
01:27:22.048 --> 01:27:24.759
<v ARSENEAULT>Well, thank you so much for talking with us.

1744
01:27:24.759 --> 01:27:25.964
And I appreciate it.

1745
01:27:25.964 --> 01:27:26.566
<v ROSAMORE>[laughs] Yeah.

1746
01:27:26.566 --> 01:27:27.470
I enjoyed this.

1747
01:27:27.470 --> 01:27:27.771
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes.

1748
01:27:27.771 --> 01:27:30.482
And I will send you a link to it.

1749
01:27:30.482 --> 01:27:32.892
And you can share it with your grandbabies.

1750
01:27:32.892 --> 01:27:33.494
<v ROSAMORE>Oh, really?

1751
01:27:33.494 --> 01:27:34.096
<v ARSENEAULT>Mm hmm.

1752
01:27:34.096 --> 01:27:35.000
<v ROSAMORE>Get out!

1753
01:27:35.000 --> 01:27:35.200
<c.p>[End Tape 4482.

1754
01:27:35.000 --> 01:27:35.200
End Session II.]
